window.RESEARCH = {"specialties": [{"id": "space-robotics", "title": "Space robotics & embodied autonomy", "domain": "Space", "hook": "Build the arm, mechanism or rover that can do useful physical work beyond Earth.", "work": "Design sensing, motion, manipulation and supervision for robotic arms, free-flyers, rovers and eventually more versatile helpers. Much of the real day is debugging sensors, testing failure recovery, comparing simulations with hardware and proving a robot can act safely under imperfect information.", "niches": ["Contact-aware robotic manipulation", "State estimation and visual navigation", "Force/torque sensing and tactile perception", "Motor drives and precision actuation", "Human-robot collaboration and teleoperation", "Learning-based control with verifiable safety limits"], "skills": ["Linear algebra, probability and differential equations", "Rigid-body dynamics, kinematics and feedback control", "C++/Python and embedded software", "Sensors, motor control and electrical debugging", "Simulation, system identification and hardware testing"], "degrees": "Aerospace with mechanisms, dynamics and controls depth fits your current direction. Mechanical engineering with robotics and spacecraft projects is an equally serious route for hands-on machine design. EE/CS teammates supply important sensing, electronics and autonomy expertise; you do not need to change to EE to work on space robots. An MS can deepen advanced robotics; a PhD is most relevant for research-heavy work.", "companies": [{"name": "MDA Space", "url": "https://mda.space/skymaker", "why": "Space robotic systems and SKYMAKER product family."}, {"name": "Apptronik", "url": "https://apptronik.com/news-collection/apptronik-unveils-apollo", "why": "Terrestrial humanoids with NASA-linked research heritage; useful adjacent employer, not evidence of deployed space humanoids."}, {"name": "NASA Ames / Johnson", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/integrated-system-for-autonomous-and-adaptive-caretaking-isaac/", "why": "Research and testing of autonomous spacecraft caretaking and dexterous robotics."}], "pros": ["Closest match to your interest in mechanisms, moving machines, assembly and space.", "Portable to factories, underwater vehicles and other autonomous machines.", "Technical depth can grow from approachable tabletop projects."], "cons": ["Advanced roles demand substantial math and careful software work.", "Space qualification and limited flight opportunities slow feedback.", "A compelling robot demo may hide difficult reliability and cost problems."], "outlook2034": "High confidence that robotics/controls remain useful; medium confidence in large-scale space-robot demand. Aim for robust perception, manipulation and fault recovery rather than one fashionable robot shape.", "outlook2040": "If orbital and lunar activity grows, unattended maintenance and construction become attractive uses. Humanoids are one architecture among several.", "confidence": "High skill durability; medium market-growth confidence", "spaceflight": "Strong relevance to supporting human missions. Usually a ground role; robotics expertise alone does not create a seat.", "starterProject": "Make a small robot or simulated arm find and move a marked object; log position error, failed grasps and recovery behavior. Later add contact sensing and a delayed communications mode.", "sourceIds": ["sp06", "sp07", "sp08", "sp09", "sp10"]}, {"id": "orbital-servicing", "title": "Orbital servicing, assembly & space logistics", "domain": "Space", "hook": "Become the mechanic of machines that were once treated as disposable.", "work": "Engineer rendezvous interfaces, capture mechanisms, inspection tools, refueling connections and assembly operations. This sits at the intersection of spacecraft dynamics, robotic contact, fluid transfer, verification and mission economics.", "niches": ["Rendezvous/proximity operations and relative navigation", "Docking/capture mechanisms and contact dynamics", "Robotic inspection and nondestructive evaluation", "Serviceable spacecraft interfaces", "On-orbit refueling and propellant gauging", "Assembly of large structures and precision metrology"], "skills": ["Orbital mechanics and relative motion", "Controls and uncertainty analysis", "Mechanism design and structural dynamics", "Interface engineering and test planning", "Reliability, operations and lifecycle cost analysis"], "degrees": "Aerospace is a natural route for dynamics/mission design; ME for docking and robotic hardware; EE/CS for navigation and control. Specialized fluids or robotics graduate work can be valuable.", "companies": [{"name": "Astroscale", "url": "https://www.astroscale.com/en/missions/adras-j", "why": "Debris inspection and proximity-operations demonstration."}, {"name": "Northrop Grumman SpaceLogistics", "url": "https://www.northropgrumman.com/what-we-do/space/space-logistics-services/space-industrial-revolution", "why": "Commercial satellite life-extension heritage and servicing development."}, {"name": "MDA Space", "url": "https://mda.space/skymaker", "why": "Robotic hardware applicable to orbital infrastructure."}], "pros": ["Tangible bridge between robotics and spacecraft.", "Existing servicing and inspection demonstrations provide evidence beyond concepts.", "Problems deepen into precise, defensible specialties."], "cons": ["Customer economics and standard interfaces matter as much as technical elegance.", "Some projects are canceled despite important engineering.", "Small market and mission-specific contracts can concentrate career risk."], "outlook2034": "Medium confidence in wider adoption; prepared, serviceable spacecraft may offer a more practical niche than heroic repair of arbitrary legacy hardware.", "outlook2040": "Large telescopes, stations and maneuverable satellites could make modular assembly and maintenance more valuable, conditional on mission scale and economics.", "confidence": "Flight demonstrations exist; scaling and employer survival uncertain", "spaceflight": "Most work is explicitly robotic. Human servicing may remain mission-specific; this is a route to space infrastructure, not an automatic route to flying.", "starterProject": "Simulate two objects approaching under sensor noise, then design a non-powered desktop docking connector and measure alignment tolerance. Keep the project civilian and focused on safe assembly.", "sourceIds": ["sp11", "sp12", "sp13", "sp14"]}, {"id": "habitats-life-support", "title": "Habitats, closed-loop life support & maintainability", "domain": "Human spaceflight", "hook": "Design a place where being alive does not depend on the next resupply ship.", "work": "Make air, water, temperature, pressure, waste handling and fault response work together. The hardest problems often involve contamination, degradation, repair access and long-duration reliability rather than dramatic exterior shapes.", "niches": ["Air revitalization and trace-contaminant control", "Water recovery and microbial monitoring", "Thermal-fluid loops and two-phase flow", "Leak detection and automated fault diagnosis", "Repairable habitat interfaces and modular systems", "Regenerative life support and resource accounting"], "skills": ["Thermodynamics, fluids and heat transfer", "Chemistry and transport processes", "Instrumentation and control", "Reliability engineering and hazard analysis", "Environmental testing and system integration"], "degrees": "ME, aerospace, chemical engineering, environmental engineering and EE contribute different pieces. Choose a physical subsystem to master; an MS can help for advanced closed-loop processes. There is no single universal 'space habitat' degree.", "companies": [{"name": "Collins Aerospace", "url": "https://www.rtx.com/collinsaerospace/what-we-do/industries/space/crewed-missions", "why": "Established life-support and crewed-system capabilities."}, {"name": "Vast", "url": "https://www.vastspace.com/updates/vast-advances-haven-1-into-integration-phase", "why": "Commercial station integration, including life support and thermal systems."}, {"name": "Axiom Space", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/commercial-space/commercial-space-stations/", "why": "Commercial station development ecosystem."}, {"name": "Sierra Space", "url": "https://www.sierraspace.com/commercial-space-stations/life-space-habitat/", "why": "Expandable habitat development and structural tests."}], "pros": ["Directly tied to your interest in living in space.", "Core fluids, controls and reliability skills transfer to Earth industries.", "High-consequence engineering rewards thoroughness and systems understanding."], "cons": ["Human spaceflight is a smaller employer base than general industrial engineering.", "Testing is lengthy, documentation-intensive and safety-critical.", "A partially closed loop is not a self-sufficient settlement."], "outlook2034": "High confidence these subsystems remain necessary wherever people fly; medium confidence in the size of commercial-station hiring.", "outlook2040": "Longer missions increase the value of autonomous diagnosis, local repair and resource efficiency. Large settlements remain speculative.", "confidence": "Established engineering need; uncertain market scale", "spaceflight": "Strong mission relevance, especially as part of broad operational competence. Most ECLSS engineers work on Earth; future maintenance-crew positions are a scenario.", "starterProject": "Build a benchtop environmental monitor for temperature, humidity and CO₂, calibrate it and analyze sensor drift. Model a sealed habitat's resource budget in software; do not build a human-occupied sealed chamber.", "sourceIds": ["sp15", "sp16", "sp17", "sp18", "sp19", "sp20", "sp21"]}, {"id": "eva-human-systems", "title": "Spacesuits, EVA & human-system engineering", "domain": "Human spaceflight", "hook": "The most believable 'Iron Man' path is a machine that lets a person work somewhere lethal.", "work": "Engineer wearable life support, mobility, interfaces, tools and operations around the limits of the human body. A spacesuit is a pressure vessel, thermal system, mobility system and workplace. Human factors adds cockpit controls, workload, visibility, procedure design and usability under stress.", "niches": ["Portable life support and wearable thermal control", "Softgoods, seals and pressure-garment mobility", "Human-in-the-loop simulation and test", "EVA tool design and crew ergonomics", "Suit displays, sensors and communications", "Human-robot interfaces and operational workload"], "skills": ["Mechanics, thermofluids and materials", "Human factors and experiment design", "Sensors, embedded systems and user interfaces", "Verification, hazard analysis and test operations", "Clear technical communication"], "degrees": "ME or aerospace for suit hardware; EE for embedded systems; industrial/human-factors engineering for human-system interaction; biomedical engineering for physiological interfaces. Graduate human-factors or biomechanics study can sharpen the fit.", "companies": [{"name": "Axiom Space", "url": "https://axiomspace.com/axiom-suit", "why": "AxEMU development."}, {"name": "Collins Aerospace", "url": "https://www.rtx.com/collinsaerospace/what-we-do/industries/space/crewed-missions", "why": "Crewed-system and portable life-support expertise."}, {"name": "NASA Johnson", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/reference/jsc-human-factors-performance/", "why": "Human-system integration, crew interfaces and testing."}], "pros": ["Connects advanced hardware directly to a human purpose.", "Combines engineering with the psychology and human-performance interests in your broader exploration.", "Useful overlap with medical devices, industrial wearables and harsh-environment equipment."], "cons": ["Few organizations build complete space suits.", "Human-rated qualification is painstaking.", "Being a suit engineer or test subject does not make someone an astronaut."], "outlook2034": "Likely continuing demand for usable, maintainable crew systems if human missions continue; individual programs and suppliers can change.", "outlook2040": "Longer surface stays could emphasize repairable suits, dust resistance, mobility assistance and coordinated human-robot work.", "confidence": "Real development programs; expansion conditional", "spaceflight": "Operational/test experience is relevant to crew work, but there is no demonstrated specialization-to-seat guarantee.", "starterProject": "Design a glove-compatible control panel and test task completion/error rates with consenting volunteers using ordinary gloves. Document the human-factors tradeoffs without pressurized or life-support hardware.", "sourceIds": ["sp22", "sp23", "sp24"]}, {"id": "lunar-construction", "title": "Lunar construction, mobility & local-resource engineering", "domain": "Space", "hook": "Before a city on the Moon, somebody has to make the ground stop destroying the machines.", "work": "Develop robots, mechanisms and processes for terrain access, material handling, landing-site preparation and extraction of useful resources. Lunar dust, abrasive soil, vacuum, darkness and limited power connect construction engineering to planetary science.", "niches": ["Terramechanics and wheel-soil interaction", "Dust-tolerant seals, bearings and mechanisms", "Autonomous excavation and material handling", "Regolith processing and oxygen extraction", "Landing pads, berms and construction metrology", "Surface logistics and robotic fleet operations"], "skills": ["Mechanics, materials and soil behavior", "Robotics and off-road autonomy", "Heat transfer and process engineering", "Power/resource budgeting", "Field testing and failure analysis"], "degrees": "ME, aerospace, civil/geotechnical, materials, mining or chemical engineering can each lead to a valuable niche. For robots, prioritize mechatronics and controls; for extraction, prioritize materials/process science.", "companies": [{"name": "ICON", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/stmd/nasa-enables-construction-technology-for-moon-and-mars-exploration/", "why": "Planetary autonomous-construction research and Earth analogs."}, {"name": "Blue Origin", "url": "https://www.blueorigin.com/news/blue-alchemist-hits-major-milestone-toward-permanent-sustainable-lunar-infrastructure", "why": "Blue Alchemist resource-processing development."}, {"name": "Astrolab / Lunar Outpost", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/missions/moon-base/nasa-provides-progress-update-on-moon-base-rovers/", "why": "Current lunar rover development and NASA task orders."}], "pros": ["Exceptionally close to the 'building civilization' theme.", "Combines robots with real material and terrain problems.", "Can be approached through terrestrial construction, mining and field robotics."], "cons": ["Highly dependent on lunar transport, power and customer budgets.", "Some 'lunar construction' work remains Earth testing and prototypes.", "A degree built around only one lunar program would be fragile."], "outlook2034": "Credible specialized development and testing work; sustained industrial operations are not assured. Keep a strong terrestrial engineering base.", "outlook2040": "If surface activity expands, maintenance, material handling and process scale-up may become more valuable than spectacular one-off demonstrations.", "confidence": "Medium confidence in R&D need; low confidence in industrial scale/timing", "spaceflight": "A future field-engineer role is plausible but speculative. Robots may do most construction before crews arrive.", "starterProject": "Measure a small rover's slip and power use on safe sand/gravel test beds; compare predictions with data. Later study dust-resistant mechanism concepts and autonomous mapping.", "sourceIds": ["sp25", "sp26", "sp27", "sp28"]}, {"id": "microgravity-manufacturing", "title": "Microgravity manufacturing & automated orbital laboratories", "domain": "Space", "hook": "Build a factory whose location is part of its manufacturing process.", "work": "Design and operate compact automated payloads that heat, mix, grow, inspect or process materials in microgravity, then return products or data. The spacecraft is only one part; repeatability, product quality and customer value decide whether a demonstration becomes a business.", "niches": ["Automated pharmaceutical crystallization", "Microfluidics and laboratory robotics", "Optical/material processing and in-situ inspection", "Process instrumentation and closed-loop control", "Payload thermal systems and contamination control", "Reentry payload integration and product-quality verification"], "skills": ["Controls, instrumentation and automation", "Thermofluids and materials/process science", "Statistics and experimental design", "Cleanliness, contamination and quality systems", "Spacecraft/payload integration"], "degrees": "Aerospace or ME for payload integration; EE for instrumentation/automation; chemical/materials/biomedical engineering for the product process. The most valuable combination may cross departments rather than sit inside an aerospace catalog.", "companies": [{"name": "Varda Space Industries", "url": "https://www.varda.com/mission/w-1", "why": "Returned orbital pharmaceutical-processing demonstration."}, {"name": "Redwire", "url": "https://rdw.com/capabilities/research-and-manufacturing/", "why": "Microgravity research/manufacturing hardware and ISS operations."}], "pros": ["Outside-the-box connection between space, robotics and health.", "Automation and process-control skills remain useful on Earth.", "Real orbital experiments and returns already exist."], "cons": ["Manufacturing in orbit must outperform terrestrial alternatives after launch, return and quality costs.", "Scientific effects do not automatically imply profitable production.", "Many jobs need process or materials expertise beyond a standard aerospace degree."], "outlook2034": "Expect selective niches and experiments rather than assume vast orbital factories. Follow repeat customers, throughput and product validation.", "outlook2040": "Could broaden if valuable products and economical transport are established; autonomous production may grow faster than human factory staffing.", "confidence": "Demonstrated experiments; commercial breadth uncertain", "spaceflight": "Automation often reduces the need for human attendance. A payload specialist opportunity depends on a specific funded mission.", "starterProject": "Build a safe automated benchtop experiment with temperature logging and repeatability analysis; learn experimental design and uncertainty before attempting complex payloads.", "sourceIds": ["sp29", "sp30"]}, {"id": "crew-operations", "title": "Human-spaceflight operations, integration & test", "domain": "Human spaceflight", "hook": "Be the person who can make a complicated vehicle work when its checklist stops being enough.", "work": "Translate design intent into procedures, training, simulations and real operations. Diagnose anomalies, coordinate across subsystems, validate interfaces and understand how a crew can use or repair equipment. Technical depth supports broad operational judgment.", "niches": ["Crew training and simulation engineering", "Flight-control systems and anomaly response", "EVA and robotic operations planning", "Human-rated vehicle integration and verification", "Fault-management design and operational autonomy", "Experiment operations and crew time optimization"], "skills": ["One strong engineering subsystem foundation", "Systems thinking and interface management", "Telemetry analysis and troubleshooting", "Test planning and disciplined procedures", "Communication, teamwork and decision-making under uncertainty"], "degrees": "Aerospace, ME, EE, biomedical engineering or other relevant STEM degrees can fit. Develop a concrete technical strength before relying on a generic systems label. A qualifying MS satisfies the educational component of NASA’s current criteria; recheck future selection calls if astronaut selection remains a goal.", "companies": [{"name": "SpaceX", "url": "https://www.spacex.com/launches/polarisdawn", "why": "Crewed spacecraft and operational engineering; Polaris provides actual employee-flight examples."}, {"name": "Axiom Space", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/commercial-space/commercial-space-stations/", "why": "Commercial human-spaceflight and station ecosystem."}, {"name": "Vast", "url": "https://www.vastspace.com/updates/vast-advances-haven-1-into-integration-phase", "why": "Human-spaceflight station integration and testing."}, {"name": "NASA / mission contractors", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/esdmd/hhp/human-systems-engineering/", "why": "Integration, verification and human-spaceflight operations."}], "pros": ["Close to real vehicles, crews and mission decisions.", "A strong complement to technical specialization.", "Commercial flight examples make the ambition concrete."], "cons": ["Shift work, simulations and procedure maintenance can dominate.", "Flight opportunities remain rare and mission-specific.", "Strong interpersonal performance is essential alongside technical ability."], "outlook2034": "Likely needed across ongoing crewed programs; the number and location of employers depends on station and transport development.", "outlook2040": "More autonomous operations could reduce routine staffing while increasing the value of integration, verification and exception-handling expertise.", "confidence": "Established ground career; speculative expansion in flying roles", "spaceflight": "A relevant context with demonstrated commercial flight examples. Engineering and operations experience can support a crew application, but no reliable selection-odds ranking exists and most work remains on Earth.", "starterProject": "Run a student robot/vehicle project as a mission: write requirements and procedures, hold a test-readiness review, inject safe faults and document response decisions.", "sourceIds": ["sp04", "sp05", "sp18", "sp19", "sp23", "sp24"]}, {"id": "spacecraft-mechanisms", "title": "Spacecraft mechanisms, tribology & deployables", "domain": "Build the hardware", "hook": "The hinge that works once—and the bearing that must work for fifteen years.", "work": "Design the moving hardware inside spacecraft: joints, latches, actuators, pointing assemblies and folded structures. Tribology studies friction, wear and lubrication. This is a particularly strong match for your fascination with gears, fasteners, motion and assembly.", "niches": ["Mechanisms → precision actuation → pointing gimbals and filter wheels", "Deployables → compact packaging → solar arrays, booms and antennas", "Tribology → bearings and lubrication → long-life motion in vacuum", "Hardware verification → tolerance and thermal effects → deployment reliability"], "skills": ["CAD and tolerancing", "Machine design", "Dynamics", "Materials and tribology", "Thermal effects", "Assembly and instrumented testing"], "degrees": "AE with mechanical design, structures and robotics electives; ME is equally natural. Build practical fabrication and test depth alongside the degree.", "companies": [{"name": "Moog", "url": "https://www.moog.com/products/space-mechanisms.html", "why": "Space actuators, gimbals, array drives and optical mechanisms"}, {"name": "NASA and ESA technology teams", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/structures-materials-and-mechanisms/", "why": "Mechanism research, qualification and mission integration; public research employers"}], "pros": ["Direct match to hands-on mechanical interests", "Useful across satellites, exploration and robotics", "Small components can determine whole-mission success"], "cons": ["Long qualification cycles and extensive documentation", "Subtle friction, contamination or thermal problems", "Specialized test equipment can limit independent experimentation"], "outlook2034": "Judgment: durable demand because existing spacecraft already need dependable movement; no lunar-city boom is required.", "outlook2040": "Judgment: larger deployables and serviceable infrastructure could expand the work, conditional on funded missions.", "confidence": "High for enduring relevance; uncertain hiring volume.", "spaceflight": "Mainly Earth-based design and test. Maintenance and operations experience could complement crew candidacy, but this specialty does not provide a seat.", "starterProject": "Build a small nonhazardous folding panel or gripper; measure backlash and repeatability, then compare alternative joints and tolerances.", "sourceIds": ["ex-mechanisms-nasa", "ex-tribology-esa", "ex-moog"]}, {"id": "aviation-propulsion", "title": "Aircraft propulsion, electrification & certification", "domain": "Flight systems", "hook": "Make the next aircraft efficient enough to operate—and trustworthy enough to certify.", "work": "Improve turbines and propulsion integration, develop hybrid-electric powertrains, or demonstrate that a new aircraft satisfies its safety requirements. These are related career branches: you would normally deepen one craft rather than master all three immediately.", "niches": ["Gas turbines → turbomachinery → aerothermal performance and durability", "Aircraft integration → propulsion-airframe interaction → efficiency and noise", "Hybrid-electric systems → mass and heat management → powertrain integration", "Airworthiness → compliance evidence → verification and flight-test engineering"], "skills": ["Fluid mechanics and thermodynamics", "Heat transfer", "Structures and dynamics", "Power-system literacy", "Experimental methods", "Requirements and verification"], "degrees": "AE is a direct route for propulsion and aircraft integration; ME for engines and thermal hardware; EE for electrical powertrain components.", "companies": [{"name": "CFM International", "url": "https://www.cfmaeroengines.com/rise", "why": "Next-generation engine technology development"}, {"name": "GE Aerospace", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/armd/iasp/epfd/", "why": "Hybrid-electric demonstration partnership documented by NASA"}, {"name": "magniX", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/armd/iasp/epfd/", "why": "Electric propulsion technology and demonstration partnership"}], "pros": ["Large aviation sector alongside new technology", "Strong fit for flight, hardware and experiments", "Certification and integration skills transfer across aircraft designs"], "cons": ["Long development and certification schedules", "New propulsion architecture may not win commercially", "Cost, range, weight and infrastructure constrain adoption"], "outlook2034": "Judgment: conventional efficiency work remains useful while electrification matures. CFM targets possible late-2030s engine availability; NASA's EPFD project concluded in 2026.", "outlook2040": "Judgment: expect a mix of improved turbines and selected electric/hybrid applications; universal battery-powered long-haul flight is not a sound career assumption.", "confidence": "High for aviation engineering; medium for particular propulsion transitions.", "spaceflight": "Very indirect. Flight-test experience can be relevant to some crew profiles, but ordinary aircraft engineering is not astronaut selection.", "starterProject": "Model aircraft energy and mass tradeoffs, then validate a low-power fan or cooling experiment with measured data.", "sourceIds": ["ex-rise", "ex-epfd", "ex-faa"]}, {"id": "gnc", "title": "Guidance, navigation & control", "domain": "Intelligent machines", "hook": "Make a vehicle know where it is, choose a path, and move correctly—even when GPS and human help disappear.", "work": "Turn uncertain sensor measurements into estimates of position, velocity, and orientation; design feedback that keeps the machine stable; then prove its behavior in simulation and hardware tests. This is one of the clearest bridges between spacecraft, aircraft, and mobile robots.", "niches": ["State estimation and sensor fusion: combining cameras, inertial sensors, and other measurements without trusting any one sensor blindly.", "Relative navigation and rendezvous: understanding the motion between two spacecraft.", "Attitude control and precision pointing: keeping a telescope, antenna, or laser aimed correctly.", "Trajectory optimization: finding feasible paths within time, energy, and vehicle constraints.", "Robust and fault-tolerant control: maintaining acceptable behavior when a model is imperfect or hardware degrades.", "Verification of learning-enabled control: deciding when a learned component can be trusted inside a larger control system."], "skills": ["Linear algebra, differential equations, probability", "Dynamics, control theory, numerical optimization", "Python/C++, simulation, embedded implementation", "Experiments, uncertainty analysis, test design"], "degrees": "Aerospace, mechanical, electrical, or robotics preparation can work. Aerospace emphasizes vehicle dynamics and flight; EE often emphasizes signals and estimation. A focused master's can deepen controls, but demonstrated implementation matters too.", "companies": [{"name": "Draper", "url": "https://www.draper.com/market-areas/space-systems/space-navigation-and-isam", "why": "Space navigation, vision-based landing, and autonomous-system work."}, {"name": "Rocket Lab", "url": "https://rocketlabcorp.com/space-systems/satellite-components/star-trackers/", "why": "Spacecraft sensors and flight systems, including star trackers."}], "pros": ["Excellent match for your robotics interest with paths into many vehicle types.", "A deep mathematical specialty that still rewards real hardware skill."], "cons": ["Much of the job is modeling, debugging, and verification rather than dramatic flight tests.", "Advanced roles can demand substantial mathematics; simulation success alone is insufficient."], "evidenceNow": "NASA's 2026 GNC survey includes established sensors/actuators and developing rendezvous and proximity-operation capabilities. Draper publicly describes vision-based navigation and autonomy work.", "outlook2034": "Strong baseline relevance: every maneuvering vehicle still needs estimation and control. A particularly attractive combination is GNC plus embedded software and hardware-in-the-loop testing.", "outlook2040": "Conditional upside: fleets of servicing robots, surface vehicles, and autonomous infrastructure increase the need for reliable coordination. Even if those markets develop slowly, the core skill remains useful.", "confidence": "High for enduring technical relevance; medium for the size and timing of new space markets.", "spaceflight": "Ground-based by default. Flight-operations experience can complement a future astronaut application, but being a GNC engineer does not allocate a seat.", "starterProject": "After introductory programming and mechanics, simulate a satellite or balancing robot with noisy sensors. Compare a simple controller against disturbances, record failures, and later connect the simulation to a small safe hardware rig.", "sourceIds": ["co-gnc", "co-draper", "co-startrackers"]}, {"id": "avionics", "title": "Avionics & resilient flight computing", "domain": "Intelligent machines", "hook": "Build the computer that must keep thinking when radiation flips a bit and rebooting at the wrong moment is unacceptable.", "work": "Design and integrate flight electronics and embedded software. The specialty lives at the intersection of compute performance, radiation effects, power, timing, interfaces, and fault recovery. Space robotics depends on this layer underneath the autonomy algorithm.", "niches": ["Radiation effects and assurance: understanding how the mission environment can damage electronics or corrupt state.", "Fault-tolerant computing: error detection, recovery, redundant architectures, and safe modes.", "FPGA and ASIC design: custom digital hardware with tightly controlled timing and power.", "Real-time flight software: predictable response under competing tasks and limited resources.", "Electronic packaging and interconnect reliability: making boards, connectors, and assemblies survive.", "Edge autonomy integration: fitting perception and decision software into a dependable spacecraft computer."], "skills": ["Circuits, digital logic, computer architecture", "C/C++, real-time systems, hardware debugging", "FPGA tools and test instrumentation", "Reliability, radiation fundamentals, interface design"], "degrees": "EE or computer engineering provides a direct foundation; aerospace plus substantial electronics and embedded work can also fit. Chip/device research may benefit from graduate study.", "companies": [{"name": "Microchip", "url": "https://www.microchip.com/en-us/about/news-releases/products/microchip-achieves-key-program-milestones-for-pic64-hpsc-spaceflight-computing-series", "why": "HPSC space-computing hardware and its ecosystem."}, {"name": "BAE Systems", "url": "https://www.baesystems.com/en-us/who-we-are/electronic-systems/c4isr/space-electronics", "why": "Radiation-hardened processors, ASICs, packaging, and assurance."}], "pros": ["A very strong bridge from your earlier EE direction to spacecraft and robots.", "Skills also apply to industrial, aviation, automotive, and other demanding embedded systems."], "cons": ["Qualification and debugging can be slow and painstaking.", "A faster terrestrial chip is not automatically a suitable flight computer; novelty must be balanced against evidence."], "evidenceNow": "NASA's 2026 HPSC project record and Microchip's October 2026 milestone report show continuing investment in performance, power, interoperability, and fault tolerance.", "outlook2034": "Strong candidate if you enjoy both hardware and software. Autonomous missions will need engineers who can connect sophisticated algorithms to trustworthy electronics.", "outlook2040": "Likely durable even as AI tools improve: architecture choices, environmental validation, and responsibility for failure behavior remain physical engineering problems. Exact processor families will change.", "confidence": "High for continuing need; medium for which architectures and employers dominate.", "spaceflight": "Usually designed, qualified, and supported from Earth. Operational troubleshooting knowledge can be relevant to crew work, but the job itself is not a flight pathway.", "starterProject": "Build a small environmental data logger with a watchdog, persistent logs, and controlled fault injection. Explain how it detects a sensor failure and recovers without hiding the failure.", "sourceIds": ["co-hpsc", "co-microchip", "co-bae"]}, {"id": "space-power", "title": "Space power & energy systems", "domain": "Space infrastructure", "hook": "Every robot, habitat, transmitter, and electric engine eventually asks the same question: where does the next watt come from?", "work": "Design generation, storage, conversion, and distribution so a mission can keep operating through eclipses, degradation, peak loads, and faults. A space power engineer may work on a solar cell, a battery controller, a converter, or the architecture of the entire electrical system.", "niches": ["Space photovoltaics: semiconductor cells, radiation degradation, and array design.", "Power electronics: converting and regulating electricity with low mass and acceptable heat.", "Battery management and lifetime: balancing energy, temperature, safety, and degradation.", "Power management and distribution: deciding which loads receive power and how faults are isolated.", "Deployable arrays: the electrical-mechanical interface between folded launch hardware and operational power.", "Surface microgrids: a future-facing niche in connecting multiple lunar assets and storage systems."], "skills": ["Circuits, power electronics, controls", "Energy budgets and thermal modeling", "Battery and semiconductor fundamentals", "Hardware tests and fault analysis"], "degrees": "EE is especially direct for conversion and distribution; materials/applied physics for solar cells; mechanical/aerospace for deployable structures and thermal integration.", "companies": [{"name": "Rocket Lab / SolAero", "url": "https://rocketlabcorp.com/space-systems/solar/", "why": "Space solar cells, arrays, manufacturing, and qualification."}, {"name": "Redwire", "url": "https://rdw.com/product-archive/power/", "why": "Deployable power hardware and roll-out solar arrays."}], "pros": ["Useful across almost every space mission and many terrestrial industries.", "Combines your civilization-building interest with a concrete, measurable bottleneck."], "cons": ["The exciting mission may depend on years of component qualification and incremental efficiency gains.", "Large lunar grids and orbital industry require customers and infrastructure beyond the power hardware itself."], "evidenceNow": "The NASA 2026 power survey treats generation, storage, and distribution as a fundamental spacecraft subsystem. Rocket Lab and Redwire already supply flight hardware.", "outlook2034": "High baseline value in satellites, spacecraft, and robotics. Power conversion plus controls plus thermal awareness is a versatile specialization stack.", "outlook2040": "Potential expansion into surface grids and higher-power orbital infrastructure, conditional on deployment scale. These are stronger reasons to learn power engineering than a specific promise about space data centers.", "confidence": "High for core spacecraft demand; medium for surface infrastructure; low for specific future market volumes.", "spaceflight": "Most work stays on Earth. Maintaining power systems could be important crew work in future habitats, but routine orbital power-engineer staffing is not established.", "starterProject": "Create a simulated spacecraft power budget through sunlight and eclipse. Later build a low-voltage solar-powered sensor with measured energy use and automatic load shedding.", "sourceIds": ["co-power", "co-solar", "co-redwire-power"]}, {"id": "thermal-cryogenic", "title": "Thermal engineering & cryogenic fluids", "domain": "Space infrastructure", "hook": "Keep a computer warm enough to work, a habitat comfortable, and propellant cold enough to remain liquid—all on the same mission.", "work": "Predict and control heat flow, then test whether the hardware behaves as predicted. Cryogenic specialization adds very cold fluids, boil-off, storage, and transfer. Ordinary spacecraft thermal control is established; large-scale orbital cryogenic logistics is a developing application.", "niches": ["Thermal modeling and model correlation: reconciling predictions with thermal-vacuum test data.", "Heat pipes, fluid loops, and radiators: transporting heat and rejecting it to space.", "Cryocoolers and instrument cooling: keeping sensitive detectors at their operating temperatures.", "Cryogenic storage and transfer: managing heat leaks, fluid behavior, and interfaces in microgravity.", "Two-phase flow: systems where liquid and vapor interact.", "Coupled power/thermal design: preventing a high-power device from becoming unusable waste heat."], "skills": ["Thermodynamics, heat transfer, fluid mechanics", "Numerical modeling and instrumentation", "Vacuum and cryogenic test methods", "Uncertainty, materials, and system interfaces"], "degrees": "Mechanical or aerospace is a common route; chemical engineering can fit fluid/thermal processes. EE plus thermal depth is valuable at power-electronics interfaces.", "companies": [{"name": "Eta Space", "url": "https://etaspace.com/news", "why": "Cryogenic storage, transfer, and cooling development."}, {"name": "Advanced Cooling Technologies", "url": "https://www.1-act.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/SPC-BRO_Space-Brochure_V2_2026-02-13_Web-1.pdf", "why": "Spacecraft thermal design, hardware, and qualification."}, {"name": "Honeywell Aerospace", "url": "https://www.honeywellaerospace.com/us/en/products-and-services/products/emerging-technologies/space/control-and-life-support", "why": "Cryocoolers and environmental-control products."}], "pros": ["A less glamorous specialty that can control whether the whole mission works.", "Excellent fallback relevance in energy, electronics, refrigeration, and advanced manufacturing."], "cons": ["Testing needs specialized facilities; progress can be constrained by hardware schedules.", "A depot business can fail even when its thermal technology works."], "evidenceNow": "NASA documents both established thermal-control methods and developing compact active systems. Its 2026 LOXSAT material describes cryogenic storage/transfer demonstration work, not a completed depot network.", "outlook2034": "One of the strongest overlooked paths for your interests. Space vehicles, electronics, and habitats all retain thermal constraints regardless of which flagship program wins.", "outlook2040": "Potentially central to refueling, lunar operations, and industrial processing. Larger power systems make heat rejection more demanding; the scale of those markets remains conditional.", "confidence": "High for thermal relevance; medium for orbital cryogenic-logistics scale.", "spaceflight": "Normally Earth-based analysis and testing. Thermal-fluid and life-support troubleshooting could complement an operator profile, without guaranteeing flight selection.", "starterProject": "Measure heating and cooling of a safe low-voltage electronic enclosure. Build a simple model, compare predictions with measurements, and explain why the model disagrees. Cryogenic experimentation belongs in a supervised laboratory.", "sourceIds": ["co-thermal", "co-loxsat", "co-eta", "co-act"]}, {"id": "chemical-propulsion", "title": "Chemical propulsion & engine development", "domain": "Flight systems", "hook": "Turn a difficult-to-control energy release into repeatable, efficient vehicle motion.", "work": "Develop rocket engines and their supporting machinery, structures, instrumentation, and test programs. Chemical propulsion includes high-thrust launch engines and smaller spacecraft thrusters; they demand different balances of performance, storage, reliability, and cost.", "niches": ["Combustion and stability: understanding coupled flow, acoustics, and energy release.", "Turbomachinery: pumps, turbines, seals, bearings, and rotating dynamics.", "Cooling and hot-section durability: protecting hardware through severe thermal cycles.", "Valves, feed systems, and controls: making the whole propulsion system behave predictably.", "Manufacturing and acceptance testing: producing repeatable engines at useful cost.", "Reuse and health monitoring: understanding degradation between flights."], "skills": ["Fluid mechanics, thermodynamics, chemistry", "Solid mechanics, vibration, materials", "Instrumentation and data analysis", "Simulation plus disciplined laboratory testing"], "degrees": "Aerospace or mechanical is common; chemical engineering, materials, and EE contribute in specific subfields. Research-intensive combustion and turbomachinery roles may favor graduate specialization.", "companies": [{"name": "Blue Origin", "url": "https://www.blueorigin.com/engines", "why": "Launch, upper-stage, and lunar-engine development."}, {"name": "Rocketdyne", "url": "https://rocketdyne.com/", "why": "Space propulsion and power hardware; verify current business structure when applying."}], "pros": ["Direct connection to launch vehicles and physically impressive hardware.", "Deep, tangible engineering with many valuable component-level specialties."], "cons": ["Facilities, location, schedules, and test campaigns strongly shape the job.", "A narrow attachment to one engine architecture or employer can limit flexibility."], "evidenceNow": "Blue Origin's current portfolio spans launch and lunar engines. NASA's propulsion survey distinguishes rapid/high-thrust chemical maneuvers from low-thrust electric applications.", "outlook2034": "Still a credible specialty. Reusability, production rate, reliability, and cost can matter as much as headline performance.", "outlook2040": "Chemical engines can remain valuable alongside electric and nuclear systems because missions have different thrust requirements. The risk is program concentration, not a simple expiration date for the physics.", "confidence": "High for continuing physical relevance; medium for employer and program distribution.", "spaceflight": "Engineers generally develop and operate test systems on Earth. Propulsion knowledge supports spacecraft operations but is not a direct crew assignment.", "starterProject": "Begin with an engine-cycle comparison using published educational data, emphasizing energy balance and uncertainty. Join a supervised university propulsion team once the foundations and safety training are in place.", "sourceIds": ["co-propulsion", "co-blue-engines", "co-rocketdyne", "co-rampt"]}, {"id": "electric-propulsion", "title": "Electric propulsion & plasma systems", "domain": "Flight systems", "hook": "Spend less propellant by accelerating it with electricity—and accept that the push may be gentle and continuous.", "work": "Develop electric thrusters and the electrical, fluid, thermal, and spacecraft interfaces that let them operate reliably for long periods. This is propulsion with a strong EE and applied-physics flavor.", "niches": ["Hall-effect and ion propulsion: plasma generation and controlled acceleration.", "Power processing units: converting spacecraft electrical power into what a thruster needs.", "Cathodes and lifetime: understanding wear, erosion, and long-duration behavior.", "Feed systems and alternative propellants: balancing storage, compatibility, supply, and performance.", "Plume interactions: understanding how the thruster affects nearby spacecraft surfaces.", "Low-thrust mission integration: pairing propulsion capability with orbit and trajectory design."], "skills": ["Electromagnetism and plasma fundamentals", "Power electronics and controls", "Vacuum testing and diagnostics", "Thermal analysis, simulation, and long-duration data"], "degrees": "Aerospace, EE, mechanical, or applied physics can fit. A master's or PhD can be useful for plasma research; product and test work also needs engineers with strong practical foundations.", "companies": [{"name": "Safran Spacecraft Propulsion", "url": "https://www.safran-group.com/companies/safran-spacecraft-propulsion", "why": "Hall thrusters, feed systems, and power-processing subsystems."}, {"name": "Exotrail", "url": "https://www.exotrail.com/our-technology", "why": "Miniaturized Hall-effect propulsion and satellite mobility."}], "pros": ["A particularly interesting bridge between your early EE plan and aerospace propulsion.", "Already a real spacecraft technology, with room for work on lifetime, integration, and cost."], "cons": ["Electric propulsion is not a universal replacement for high-thrust launch engines.", "Specialized vacuum facilities and long endurance tests can constrain work."], "evidenceNow": "NASA's 2026 survey describes multiple electric-propulsion families and emphasizes device-specific maturity. Safran sells Hall-thruster subsystems today.", "outlook2034": "Good technical relevance for maneuverable satellites and in-space transport. Power electronics and test capability strengthen portability beyond a single thruster type.", "outlook2040": "Higher-power transport and deeper-space applications are plausible if power generation and heat rejection progress. Forecasts should track the complete vehicle, not thruster efficiency alone.", "confidence": "High for existing in-space applications; medium for expansion into larger transport systems.", "spaceflight": "Predominantly Earth-based research, manufacturing, and mission support.", "starterProject": "Compare published chemical and electric propulsion mission tradeoffs in a simple simulation. Explore how available power and thrust change travel time without treating a conceptual model as a flight design.", "sourceIds": ["co-propulsion", "co-safran", "co-exotrail"]}, {"id": "nuclear-space", "title": "Space nuclear power & propulsion", "domain": "Frontier bets", "hook": "Give a machine useful power where sunlight is weak, interrupted, or insufficient.", "work": "Integrate a space energy source with conversion, heat rejection, structures, control, and mission assurance. Nuclear electric propulsion uses a reactor to supply electricity to thrusters; nuclear thermal propulsion heats propellant directly. Radioisotope power is a different technology again.", "niches": ["Power conversion: turning heat into useful electrical output.", "Heat rejection and thermal integration: removing waste heat within mass constraints.", "Controls and instrumentation for long-duration operation.", "Materials and component qualification under demanding environments.", "Nuclear safety analysis, mission assurance, and launch approval interfaces.", "System architecture: matching source, shielding, vehicle layout, and mission needs."], "skills": ["Thermodynamics, heat transfer, electrical systems", "Nuclear-engineering fundamentals for source-specific work", "Materials, reliability, and systems analysis", "Model validation and rigorous documentation"], "degrees": "Nuclear engineering for reactor-specific roles; mechanical/aerospace for thermal-fluid and vehicle integration; EE for power conversion, controls, and instrumentation. Many advanced research paths favor graduate study.", "companies": [{"name": "Sierra Lobo", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/sr-1-freedom-advances-with-progress-on-nuclear-power-module/", "why": "NASA identifies it as the SR-1 power-conversion supplier in October 2026."}, {"name": "General Atomics", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/space-technology-mission-directorate/tdm/space-nuclear-propulsion/", "why": "NASA identifies nuclear-thermal development contract work."}, {"name": "Standard Nuclear", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/space-technology-mission-directorate/tdm/space-nuclear-propulsion/", "why": "NASA reports 2025 nuclear-thermal work following acquisition of Ultra Safe Nuclear Technologies."}], "pros": ["High intellectual depth and a direct connection to ambitious long-duration exploration.", "Thermal, power, controls, and materials skills can transfer to terrestrial energy work."], "cons": ["A small, policy-sensitive market with concentrated employers and specialized facilities.", "Program announcements can change before flight; avoid making one mission your entire career plan."], "evidenceNow": "NASA's October 20, 2026 SR-1 update identifies a reactor/power-conversion development effort targeting a late-2028 nuclear-electric flight. That is a current plan, not an achieved interplanetary demonstration.", "outlook2034": "An intriguing specialist option if real flight results and sustained funding accumulate. A broad power/thermal foundation gives you a stronger fallback than specializing only in one proposed reactor mission.", "outlook2040": "Potentially important for deep-space transport and surface infrastructure, but this has greater program uncertainty than ordinary spacecraft power, avionics, or thermal engineering.", "confidence": "Medium for continuing R&D; low-to-medium for the size of a commercial space-nuclear labor market.", "spaceflight": "No inherent route to personally flying. Most expertise is applied in Earth-based design, qualification, and operations support.", "starterProject": "Make a non-nuclear system-level comparison of solar versus continuous electrical supply for a fictional deep-space payload, including power conversion and radiator mass assumptions. Study the energy balance before any highly specialized technology.", "sourceIds": ["co-nuclear", "co-ntp"]}, {"id": "aerodynamics-entry", "title": "Aerodynamics, aerothermodynamics & reentry", "domain": "Flight systems", "hook": "Make a vehicle survive the atmosphere—and use the atmosphere to do useful work.", "work": "Predict and measure fluid flow, aerodynamic loads, heating, and flight behavior. Aircraft aerodynamics and atmospheric-entry work share foundations but branch into different regimes. Reentry couples a trajectory, a heat shield, structure, control, and landing system.", "niches": ["Computational fluid dynamics: numerical prediction checked against physical evidence.", "Experimental aerodynamics: wind-tunnel measurements, sensors, and uncertainty.", "Aerothermal environments: the combined fluid and heating conditions around fast vehicles.", "Thermal protection materials: understanding recession, damage, and reuse limits.", "Entry, descent, and landing: integrating deceleration, stability, navigation, and landing.", "Aeroelasticity and loads: interaction between airflow and flexible structures."], "skills": ["Fluid mechanics, thermodynamics, numerical methods", "Differential equations and scientific computing", "Experimental measurements and statistics", "Dynamics, materials, and coupled-system modeling"], "degrees": "Aerospace is a direct route; mechanical also fits with fluids depth. Advanced CFD, turbulence, and high-temperature gas research often benefit from graduate study.", "companies": [{"name": "Varda", "url": "https://www.varda.com/mission/w-5", "why": "Recoverable capsules and commercial reentry/heat-shield work."}, {"name": "Blue Origin", "url": "https://www.blueorigin.com/new-glenn", "why": "Reusable launch vehicles with aerodynamic descent control."}], "pros": ["A direct match for your fascination with flying vehicles and returning from space.", "Strong physical insight with applications in aviation, space, and other fluid-flow industries."], "cons": ["High-fidelity simulation can be computationally expensive and still require difficult validation.", "A specialty such as planetary entry is narrower than general fluid/thermal engineering."], "evidenceNow": "NASA's EDL program combines analysis, test, and flight work; LOFTID demonstrated an inflatable entry aeroshell in 2022. Varda offers a current commercial reentry example.", "outlook2034": "Credible relevance wherever reusable vehicles, payload return, or atmospheric landing are being developed. The strongest profile pairs modeling with instrumentation and test interpretation.", "outlook2040": "Larger planetary payloads and reusable logistics could create demanding new problems. Do not assume a crewed Mars-landing schedule; aircraft and Earth-return applications offer nearer foundations.", "confidence": "High for continuing aerospace need; medium for commercial return growth; lower for specific Mars architectures.", "spaceflight": "Typically analysis and test work on Earth. Flight-test experience may be operationally valuable, but designing a capsule does not make you its crew.", "starterProject": "Study a low-speed glider using a simple model and measured flight data. Later compare an educational entry simulation against published benchmark cases and quantify model limits.", "sourceIds": ["co-edl", "co-esm", "co-loftid", "co-varda", "co-newglenn"]}, {"id": "structures-manufacturing", "title": "Structures, materials & advanced manufacturing", "domain": "Build the hardware", "hook": "Make a machine light enough to launch, strong enough to survive, and repeatable enough to manufacture.", "work": "Translate a vehicle or instrument into load-bearing hardware, materials choices, joining processes, and production methods. The most valuable work often sits between design, manufacturing, and test rather than inside a single CAD model.", "niches": ["Structural dynamics and vibration: how loads move through a vehicle during launch and operation.", "Composites and lightweight structures: stiffness, damage, manufacturing variability, and inspection.", "Deployable structures and mechanisms: packing a large structure into a small launch volume.", "Additive manufacturing and process qualification: proving that a printed part is consistently fit for its purpose.", "Fracture, fatigue, and nondestructive evaluation: finding and understanding defects before failure.", "Precision structures and thermal stability: maintaining alignment as temperatures change."], "skills": ["Statics, solid mechanics, dynamics", "Materials science and manufacturing processes", "CAD, finite-element analysis, metrology", "Physical testing, inspection, and process statistics"], "degrees": "Mechanical or aerospace for structures and mechanisms; materials science for material/process depth. Manufacturing engineering is a valuable path into production and qualification.", "companies": [{"name": "Northrop Grumman", "url": "https://www.northropgrumman.com/what-we-do/space/mission-enabling-products/structures", "why": "Composite spacecraft structures and deployable supports."}, {"name": "Rocket Lab", "url": "https://rocketlabcorp.com/about/about-us/", "why": "Advanced composites, spacecraft components, and vertically integrated manufacturing."}, {"name": "Redwire", "url": "https://rdw.com/", "why": "Space structures, mechanisms, and integrated hardware."}], "pros": ["Excellent fit if you enjoy tangible machines and the factory that makes them.", "Broad portability to robotics, aviation, energy, and industrial equipment."], "cons": ["Production quality, documentation, inspection, and supplier issues occupy substantial time.", "Novel materials and printed geometries still need qualification; impressive prototypes are not enough."], "evidenceNow": "NASA's structures survey highlights compact deployables and mechanisms. RAMPT provides a concrete example of advancing manufacturing processes, materials, and test evidence for propulsion hardware.", "outlook2034": "Strong practical option: lower-cost production, reliable deployment, and repeatable quality remain useful even if space-market growth is uneven.", "outlook2040": "Potential expansion into larger orbital structures and automated assembly, conditional on transport and customers. Earth-based production expertise remains valuable across scenarios.", "confidence": "High for structural/manufacturing fundamentals; medium for large-scale orbital construction.", "spaceflight": "Usually ground manufacturing and qualification. In-space assembly or maintenance could use related expertise, but those future jobs are not yet a predictable hiring pipeline.", "starterProject": "Design a lightweight bracket or folding mechanism, predict its behavior, fabricate a safe prototype, and measure deflection or repeatability. Document the mismatch between the model and hardware.", "sourceIds": ["co-structures", "co-rampt", "co-ngstructures", "co-rocketlab", "co-redwire"]}, {"id": "rf-optical", "title": "RF, photonics & space communications", "domain": "Intelligent machines", "hook": "Connect machines across enormous distances while power, pointing, and signal quality all work against you.", "work": "Build the physical and digital links that carry commands, science data, timing, and coordination. Radio-frequency and optical links have different strengths and failure modes; future networks can use both.", "niches": ["Antennas and RF electronics: converting electrical signals into controlled electromagnetic radiation and back.", "Optical terminals and pointing: holding a narrow laser link between moving platforms.", "Signal processing and coding: recovering information from weak or noisy measurements.", "Link budgets and system architecture: understanding what range, power, data rate, and availability can coexist.", "Timing, synchronization, and navigation signals.", "Space networking and disruption tolerance: operating with intermittent links and long delays."], "skills": ["Electromagnetism, signals, probability", "RF circuits or optics/photonics", "Digital communications and embedded processing", "Test equipment, link modeling, and precision control"], "degrees": "EE is especially direct; physics/applied optics for photonics; aerospace plus communications or controls depth for system integration and pointing.", "companies": [{"name": "Mynaric / Rocket Lab", "url": "https://mynaric.com/products/space/condor-mk3/", "why": "Optical communication terminals; Rocket Lab completed its acquisition of Mynaric in April 2026."}, {"name": "Redwire", "url": "https://rdw.com/capabilities/rf-systems-and-satellite-payloads/", "why": "RF systems and satellite payload hardware."}, {"name": "Rocket Lab", "url": "https://rocketlabcorp.com/about/about-us/", "why": "Radio and spacecraft-component capabilities in the same corporate family as Mynaric."}], "pros": ["Another strong bridge between EE, space robotics, and large infrastructure.", "RF and signal-processing depth can transfer to terrestrial telecom, sensing, and instrumentation."], "cons": ["A very specialized field with significant laboratory and measurement demands.", "A successful link demonstration does not by itself establish a profitable network or guaranteed demand."], "evidenceNow": "NASA's DSOC technology demonstration completed in 2025, establishing deep-space optical links. Suppliers already market optical terminals and RF payload components.", "outlook2034": "Strong relevance to satellites and increasingly autonomous missions. Combine a physical-layer specialty with controls, embedded processing, or networking for greater flexibility.", "outlook2040": "Plausible need for higher-capacity intersatellite and deep-space links, but architecture will depend on mission economics and reliability requirements. Optical does not make radio obsolete.", "confidence": "High for communications need; medium for which optical/network architectures scale.", "spaceflight": "Predominantly Earth-based engineering and network operations. Communications expertise can help a crew operator, but it is not a selection guarantee.", "starterProject": "Build a software link-budget explorer and simulate noisy digital communication. Later use legal low-power educational hardware or receive-only SDR projects to connect the math to measurements.", "sourceIds": ["co-dsoc", "co-mynaric", "co-mynaric-acq", "co-redwire-rf", "co-rocketlab"]}, {"id": "systems-test", "title": "Systems integration, test & mission assurance", "domain": "Build the hardware", "hook": "Be the person who notices that every subsystem works alone—and the complete spacecraft still fails.", "work": "Connect requirements, interfaces, models, hardware, procedures, and test evidence into a mission that works. Some roles focus on architecture and trade studies; others on integration, environmental testing, reliability, or flight readiness. Breadth here is a technical responsibility, not the absence of expertise.", "niches": ["Requirements and interface engineering: making assumptions explicit across teams.", "Verification and validation: proving requirements are met and the actual mission need is fulfilled.", "Hardware-in-the-loop and end-to-end testing: exposing failures before flight.", "Environmental qualification: vibration, thermal vacuum, electromagnetic compatibility, and other mission conditions.", "Reliability and fault management: tracing how component failures affect the vehicle.", "Model-based systems engineering and digital models: maintaining consistent relationships between design decisions and evidence."], "skills": ["One solid technical discipline plus system-level reasoning", "Test design, statistics, data analysis", "Clear technical writing and interface management", "Debugging, configuration control, and engineering judgment"], "degrees": "Aerospace, mechanical, EE, or another relevant engineering degree can lead here. Technical depth plus real integration experience is more valuable than treating systems engineering as a substitute for fundamentals.", "companies": [{"name": "Redwire", "url": "https://rdw.com/", "why": "Current spacecraft integration/test and subsystem work."}, {"name": "Rocket Lab", "url": "https://rocketlabcorp.com/about/about-us/", "why": "Spacecraft and component development with end-to-end mission integration."}], "pros": ["A close match for your interest in whole machines and building useful systems.", "Can grow into substantial technical leadership; specialist versus generalist is not a simple pay hierarchy."], "cons": ["Some roles involve extensive coordination and documentation; inspect the actual job description.", "You must be comfortable discovering problems between disciplines and challenging assumptions."], "evidenceNow": "NASA explicitly defines systems engineering around architecture, interfaces, technical risk, and the full life cycle. ESA describes physical environmental testing and integrated spacecraft development.", "outlook2034": "Highly relevant as hardware and software become more interconnected. A good route is to develop depth in controls, electronics, thermal, or mechanisms and add integration responsibility.", "outlook2040": "Likely durable: more autonomy and more complex infrastructure increase the importance of credible evidence that the complete system works. Tools can automate artifacts while humans still own consequential engineering decisions.", "confidence": "High for enduring need; individual job quality depends strongly on actual responsibilities.", "spaceflight": "Ground integration/operations can help build a hands-on operator profile, which may support astronaut competitiveness. It does not create an ordinary route to a space job.", "starterProject": "For any small robot, write five measurable requirements, an interface diagram, and a test plan. Test the integrated machine, log failures, and distinguish meeting a requirement from satisfying the user's real need.", "sourceIds": ["co-systems", "co-verify", "co-test", "co-redwire", "co-rocketlab"]}, {"id": "underwater-robotics", "title": "Underwater robotics & ocean systems", "domain": "Ocean", "hook": "Build machines that work where GPS disappears and a leak can end the mission.", "work": "Design, integrate and test autonomous or tethered vehicles, sensor packages and subsea mechanisms. This can mean environmental science, seabed mapping, infrastructure inspection or defense. The ocean interest does not require committing to torpedoes.", "niches": ["Vehicle dynamics → underwater navigation → inertial/acoustic sensor fusion", "Mechatronics → subsea mechanisms → pressure-tolerant actuators and connectors", "Ocean sensing → underwater acoustics → sonar processing and calibration", "Operations → long-duration autonomy → fault detection and recovery"], "skills": ["Dynamics and controls", "Mechanical design and sealing concepts", "Embedded systems", "Probability and estimation", "Acoustics and signal processing", "Field testing and data analysis"], "degrees": "ME, ocean/naval engineering, EE and AE can contribute. For you, AE dynamics plus robotics projects or ME controls/mechatronics are plausible routes; targeted ocean coursework fills domain gaps.", "companies": [{"name": "HII", "url": "https://www.hii.com/capabilities/unmanned-systems", "why": "REMUS underwater vehicles and maritime autonomy"}, {"name": "Kongsberg Discovery", "url": "https://www.kongsberg.com/discovery/autonomous-and-uncrewed-solutions/auv/hugin/", "why": "HUGIN survey AUVs"}, {"name": "Anduril", "url": "https://www.anduril.com/dive-ld", "why": "Large autonomous underwater vehicles"}, {"name": "WHOI", "url": "https://www2.whoi.edu/site/osl/", "why": "Research employer developing ocean robots"}], "pros": ["Strong fit for hands-on mechanisms and complete machines", "Civil science and inspection applications provide alternatives to weapons", "Controls, autonomy and reliability skills can transfer to space robotics"], "cons": ["Sea trials, weather and deployments can disrupt schedules", "Testing access and reliable waterproof hardware can be expensive", "Aerospace knowledge needs additional ocean-specific learning"], "outlook2034": "Judgment: a relatively durable adjacent option because inspection, science and autonomous maritime systems already exist; particular hiring levels are uncertain.", "outlook2040": "Judgment: longer unattended missions and subsea infrastructure may expand the work. Widespread permanent underwater habitats are not needed for this career to make sense.", "confidence": "High for relevance of underlying skills; medium for market expansion.", "spaceflight": "Low direct connection to being selected to fly. Field operations and robotics experience may be useful evidence for later mission-operations applications, but they provide no spaceflight entitlement.", "starterProject": "Start with a simulated inspection rover or a low-voltage tethered pool robot under supervision; document sensing errors, reliability and recovery. Progress through a university robotics or ocean-engineering team.", "sourceIds": ["op-whoi", "op-remus", "op-hii", "op-kongsberg", "op-anduril"]}, {"id": "defense-engineering", "title": "Defense vehicles, integration & test", "domain": "Defense", "hook": "The career is making complex hardware trustworthy, producible and usable.", "work": "Work within multidisciplinary teams developing aircraft, missiles, sensors or naval systems. Engineers specialize in a subsystem or in integration, verification, manufacturing and sustainment. 'Weapons engineer' is an industry description, not a single undergraduate specialization.", "niches": ["Vehicle engineering → structures or thermal design → qualification and durability", "Electronics → embedded computing → environmental qualification", "Systems integration → verification → hardware/software test environments", "Manufacturing → process capability → production quality and reliability"], "skills": ["One deep engineering discipline", "Requirements and interface management", "Experimental design", "Modeling and simulation", "Reliability analysis", "Clear technical documentation"], "degrees": "AE is appropriate for flying vehicles and flight mechanics; ME for mechanisms, structures and manufacturing; EE/CE for electronics and computing. The role determines the best preparation.", "companies": [{"name": "Lockheed Martin", "url": "https://www.lockheedmartin.com/en-us/careers/why-lm/business-areas/mfc.html", "why": "Published career areas include mechanical, GNC, RF, systems and integration/test"}, {"name": "RTX", "url": "https://careers.rtx.com/global/en", "why": "Aviation and defense businesses with multiple engineering functions"}, {"name": "Northrop Grumman", "url": "https://jobs.northropgrumman.com/careers", "why": "Systems, mechanical and electronics career families"}], "pros": ["Large, established ecosystem of complex hardware programs", "Several specialties transfer to civilian aerospace", "Test and manufacturing offer tangible hardware responsibility"], "cons": ["Program priorities and budgets can shift", "Some work has citizenship or clearance constraints", "Classified projects may limit portfolio disclosure; mission fit matters personally"], "outlook2034": "Judgment: continuing demand is more plausible than weapons development becoming technically finished; no source can guarantee future budgets or an individual job.", "outlook2040": "Judgment: software integration, maintainability, production and verification remain meaningful even as platforms change. Develop a transferable craft rather than betting on one program.", "confidence": "High that engineering work continues; uncertain program and employer mix.", "spaceflight": "Most roles are Earth-based. Defense employment is not a direct route to an astronaut seat; some skills transfer to spacecraft programs and operational roles.", "starterProject": "Create a requirements and test plan for a non-weapon inspection robot in simulation. Measure failure behavior, test coverage and safe recovery, then improve the design.", "sourceIds": ["op-gao26", "op-gao25", "op-lm-careers", "op-rtx", "op-ng", "op-bls-ae"]}, {"id": "humanoids-wearables", "title": "Humanoids, dexterous robots & wearable machines", "domain": "Robotics", "hook": "Make a machine interact with the physical world—or help a human do it.", "work": "Design robot joints, transmissions, hands, sensors and control systems, then make them perform repeatable tasks. Wearables add biomechanics and comfort; medical robots add clinical requirements. These are adjacent routes into the capabilities behind your Iron Man interest.", "niches": ["Mechatronics → actuation → compact, compliant robot joints", "Manipulation → hands and grippers → tactile sensing and contact control", "Wearables → biomechanics → assistive control and ergonomics", "Human-machine systems → teleoperation → force feedback and operator interfaces"], "skills": ["Mechanisms and CAD", "Dynamics and control", "Motor and sensor integration", "Embedded programming", "Human factors", "Instrumented experimentation"], "degrees": "ME/mechatronics and EE/CE are common foundations; AE with robotics/controls electives is viable when backed by relevant projects. Graduate study can help for advanced manipulation or learning-based control research.", "companies": [{"name": "Boston Dynamics", "url": "https://bostondynamics.com/products/atlas/", "why": "Industrial humanoid development and field testing"}, {"name": "Ekso Bionics", "url": "https://eksobionics.com/en/eksonr/", "why": "Clinical rehabilitation exoskeletons"}, {"name": "Intuitive", "url": "https://www.intuitive.com/en-us/products-and-services/da-vinci", "why": "Precision surgical robotics"}, {"name": "NASA Johnson", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/technology/r5/", "why": "Humanoid research; government research employer"}], "pros": ["Excellent fit for mechanisms, motion and assembly", "Broad terrestrial applications reduce dependence on space schedules", "Clear progression from small working mechanisms to complete systems"], "cons": ["Reliable contact and manipulation are harder than polished demonstrations suggest", "General-purpose humanoid business models remain uncertain", "Medical and human-contact systems require extensive validation"], "outlook2034": "Judgment: attractive capability stack with real industrial and medical applications. Demand for useful automation is a more robust thesis than a prediction that every workplace will use humanoids.", "outlook2040": "Judgment: dexterous and wearable robots could expand in industry and some space tasks. They must be qualified for their intended environment: exterior robots face vacuum, dust and radiation; cabin robots face different constraints. Humanoid dominance is not established.", "confidence": "High for robotics skills; medium for mass humanoid adoption; low for a specific space-humanoid timeline.", "spaceflight": "Building robots usually happens on Earth. Human-robot operations or maintenance expertise could support crew candidacy, but robots can also reduce the need to send people.", "starterProject": "Build a small low-force gripper or camera-guided sorting arm. Compare predicted motion with measured motion, quantify repeatability, and document mechanical changes after testing.", "sourceIds": ["op-atlas", "op-valkyrie", "op-intuitive", "op-ekso", "op-ucsd-mae"]}, {"id": "orbital-sustainability", "title": "Orbital sustainability & space traffic", "domain": "Space", "hook": "Make a growing space economy coexist with everything already in orbit.", "work": "Track objects, estimate their motion, assess collision risk, plan operations and develop serviceable or safely disposable spacecraft. This connects astrodynamics with sensors, software, operations and sometimes robotic servicing.", "niches": ["Astrodynamics → orbit determination → uncertainty-aware conjunction assessment", "Space sensing → radar/optical tracking → sensor fusion", "Spacecraft lifecycle → design for disposal → reliability and end-of-life operations", "Servicing → non-cooperative inspection → proximity operations and mission safety"], "skills": ["Orbital mechanics", "Statistics and uncertainty", "Numerical methods", "Sensor fusion", "Simulation software", "Operational decision-making"], "degrees": "AE with astrodynamics is a direct route; EE, applied mathematics, CS and physics also contribute. Hardware-oriented students can focus on servicing mechanisms or disposal hardware instead of analytics alone.", "companies": [{"name": "Astroscale", "url": "https://www.astroscale.com/en/news/astroscales-adras-j-mission-completes-operations-begins-deorbit", "why": "Debris inspection demonstrated; removal development is a separate milestone"}, {"name": "LeoLabs", "url": "https://leolabs.space/space-traffic-management/", "why": "Commercial tracking and conjunction services"}, {"name": "Slingshot Aerospace", "url": "https://www.slingshot.space/solutions-government-and-defense", "why": "Space sensing, orbit determination and risk analysis"}, {"name": "Office of Space Commerce", "url": "https://space.commerce.gov/traffic-coordination-system-for-space-tracss/", "why": "Civil space-traffic service; government employer/ecosystem"}], "pros": ["Addresses an existing, measured constraint on space operations", "Combines meaningful space work with transferable math/software", "Interfaces naturally with servicing and space robotics"], "cons": ["Some roles are primarily data/software work", "Debris-removal economics depend on who pays and what is required", "A successful inspection mission is not proof of a mature removal business"], "outlook2034": "Judgment: tracking, collision assessment and safe operations look durable. Routine commercial debris removal is a more conditional bet.", "outlook2040": "Judgment: larger constellations and more complex operations could increase need, but regulation, coordination and economics will shape the workforce.", "confidence": "High for the safety problem; medium for business growth; uncertain for removal scale.", "spaceflight": "Low direct likelihood; most work is ground-based. Robotic servicing may be strategically important precisely because it avoids crew exposure.", "starterProject": "Use public orbital data to visualize orbit uncertainty and close approaches in an educational simulator. Label its outputs educational rather than operational collision warnings.", "sourceIds": ["op-esa-debris", "op-tracss", "op-astroscale", "op-leolabs", "op-slingshot"]}, {"id": "space-environments", "title": "Space weather & environment engineering", "domain": "Space", "hook": "Engineer for a Sun that does not care about your mission schedule.", "work": "Translate radiation and plasma environments into instrument, spacecraft and operations requirements. Some jobs build sensors; others model environments or help operators interpret forecasts and anomalies. This is a smaller field at the engineering–physics boundary.", "niches": ["Environment engineering → radiation modeling → mission-specific exposure assessment", "Scientific instruments → detectors → calibration and reliability", "Space operations → weather monitoring → risk-informed procedures", "Applied physics → plasma interactions → spacecraft charging analysis"], "skills": ["Electromagnetism and modern physics", "Probability and data analysis", "Scientific computing", "Instrumentation", "Systems requirements", "Experimental validation"], "degrees": "AE with space-environment work can fit; EE and physics are strong routes for instruments and plasma/radiation analysis. Research-heavy roles often favor advanced study. This is an option if the physical science becomes more exciting than mechanism design.", "companies": [{"name": "Airbus Defence and Space", "url": "https://www.esa.int/Space_Safety/Vigil/Vigil_mission_overview", "why": "Prime contractor for ESA's planned Vigil spacecraft"}, {"name": "RAL Space", "url": "https://www.ralspace.stfc.ac.uk/mission/vigil", "why": "Space-weather instrumentation; research employer"}, {"name": "NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center", "url": "https://www.spaceweather.gov/impacts", "why": "Operational forecasting and impacts; government employer"}], "pros": ["Connects deep physics to practical spacecraft survival", "Useful across satellites, human exploration and terrestrial infrastructure", "Instrument development can be very hands-on"], "cons": ["Smaller labor market than general mechanical or controls work", "Some paths require substantial graduate research", "Forecast precision has physical and observational limits"], "outlook2034": "Judgment: relevance persists because satellites already experience environmental effects. Vigil's planned 2031 launch is a concrete program signal, not a guaranteed future employer opportunity.", "outlook2040": "Judgment: more operations beyond low Earth orbit could deepen the need, while the underlying physics remains relevant even if exploration schedules slip.", "confidence": "High for physical relevance; medium for niche hiring expansion.", "spaceflight": "Usually ground-based science and engineering. Radiation/operations expertise could contribute to a crew skill set but is not a known privileged selection route.", "starterProject": "Analyze public solar-event and satellite-environment data, explain uncertainty, and produce a simple mission-environment dashboard. Pair it with supervised detector or sensor laboratory work later.", "sourceIds": ["op-noaa-weather", "op-vigil", "op-ral"]}, {"id": "planetary-protection", "title": "Planetary protection & contamination control", "domain": "Space", "hook": "Reach another world without ruining the evidence you went there to find.", "work": "Help keep spacecraft biologically clean and control particles or molecular films that can degrade instruments. Planetary protection and engineering contamination control overlap, but are not identical. The work spans materials, assembly, testing, procedures and scientific integrity.", "niches": ["Contamination engineering → materials behavior → instrument cleanliness budgets", "Planetary protection → bioburden assessment → mission compliance and sampling", "Assembly/integration/test → cleanroom operations → process verification", "Sample-return systems → containment assurance → interdisciplinary systems engineering"], "skills": ["Materials and chemistry foundations", "Statistics and measurement", "Cleanroom practice", "Systems engineering", "Microbiology literacy", "Careful verification and documentation"], "degrees": "AE or ME with materials/thermal/testing depth can enter contamination engineering; biology, chemistry and environmental disciplines contribute to biological protection. For a research specialist, graduate training may be valuable. Keep a broader technical capability alongside this small niche.", "companies": [{"name": "NASA/JPL", "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/videos/spacecraft-makers-how-we-keep-europa-clipper-super-clean/", "why": "Documented planetary-protection engineering team on Europa Clipper"}, {"name": "NASA safety and mission assurance ecosystem", "url": "https://sma.nasa.gov/sma-disciplines/planetary-protection", "why": "Mission standards, assessment and implementation across agency and contractor teams"}], "pros": ["Unusual specialization with a clear mission purpose", "Mixes physical hardware, laboratory work and systems thinking", "Relevant to both science credibility and instrument performance"], "cons": ["Very small, mission-dependent specialist market", "Less visible mechanical creation than robotics or propulsion", "Policies and requirements evolve with science and destinations"], "outlook2034": "Judgment: a credible niche around planetary and precision-instrument missions. Treat it as a depth layered onto materials, test or systems skills, not a guaranteed large hiring category.", "outlook2040": "Judgment: complex exploration and sample handling could create difficult new problems, conditional on funded missions. Human settlement would complicate contamination questions rather than resolve them.", "confidence": "High that the discipline exists; uncertain future mission volume.", "spaceflight": "Mostly Earth-based engineering. A future sample-handling or science-operations role might benefit from it, but no established pipeline makes specialists likely to fly.", "starterProject": "Study a real mission's cleanliness requirements and make an educational verification plan for an inert optical instrument enclosure; seek university cleanroom or materials-lab experience.", "sourceIds": ["op-jpl-protection", "op-nasa-protection", "op-jpl-team"]}], "sources": [{"id": "sp01", "title": "NASA: Become an Astronaut", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/astronauts/become-an-astronaut/", "claim": "Current eligibility: U.S. citizenship, qualifying STEM master's or listed equivalent, three years of related professional experience or qualifying pilot experience, physical qualification, and leadership/teamwork/communication. Recheck future vacancy announcements."}, {"id": "sp02", "title": "NASA: Astronaut selection FAQs", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/becoming-an-astronaut-frequently-asked-questions/", "claim": "NASA recommends no particular qualifying degree over another; flying and military experience are not mandatory. NASA states no program age restriction."}, {"id": "sp03", "title": "NASA: Astronaut Fact Book", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/reference/astronaut-fact-book/", "claim": "The 2025 class contained 10 candidates drawn from over 8,000 applications; candidate training precedes eligibility for flight assignments."}, {"id": "sp04", "title": "Polaris Program: Team", "url": "https://polarisprogram.com/team/", "claim": "Sarah Gillis and Anna Menon held space operations engineering and crew-training/mission-control responsibilities before flying on Polaris Dawn."}, {"id": "sp05", "title": "SpaceX: Polaris Dawn mission", "url": "https://www.spacex.com/launches/polarisdawn", "claim": "Polaris Dawn flew in September 2024 and conducted an EVA using SpaceX-developed suits."}, {"id": "sp06", "title": "NASA: ISAAC autonomous spacecraft caretaking", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/integrated-system-for-autonomous-and-adaptive-caretaking-isaac/", "claim": "ISAAC developed autonomous caretaking using station systems and Astrobee; uncrewed cargo transfer involving a Robonaut manipulator was demonstrated in simulation, not a deployed lunar station."}, {"id": "sp07", "title": "MDA Space: SKYMAKER", "url": "https://mda.space/skymaker", "claim": "MDA markets modular space robotics derived from its established space-robotics capabilities."}, {"id": "sp08", "title": "Canadian Space Agency: About Canadarm3", "url": "https://www.asc-csa.gc.ca/eng/canadarm3/about.asp", "claim": "Canadarm3 is being developed as a smart robotic system for Gateway; distinguish development from operational deployment."}, {"id": "sp09", "title": "NASA: Humanoid robots assist assembly lines", "url": "https://spinoff.nasa.gov/Humanoid_Robots_Assist_Assembly_Lines", "claim": "Apptronik's Apollo builds on NASA-supported humanoid research, with terrestrial industrial applications."}, {"id": "sp10", "title": "NASA: Valkyrie tested in Australia", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/johnson/nasa-humanoid-robot-to-be-tested-in-australia/", "claim": "Valkyrie work with Woodside develops remote dexterous manipulation for uncrewed/offshore facilities, providing a real bridge between terrestrial industrial and space robotics."}, {"id": "sp11", "title": "Astroscale: ADRAS-J", "url": "https://www.astroscale.com/en/missions/adras-j", "claim": "ADRAS-J completed the inspection phase of JAXA's CRD2 project and demonstrated proximity operations around existing debris; this is not equivalent to having removed that debris."}, {"id": "sp12", "title": "Northrop Grumman: Space Industrial Revolution", "url": "https://www.northropgrumman.com/what-we-do/space/space-logistics-services/space-industrial-revolution", "claim": "MEV missions have docked with commercial satellites for life extension; refueling and additional servicing capabilities are separate development efforts."}, {"id": "sp13", "title": "NASA: ISAM State of Play 2026", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/commercial-space/nasas-isam-state-of-play-the-industry-standard-for-space-servicing-assembly-and-manufacturing/", "claim": "The 2026 reference catalogs physically existing prototypes and flight demonstrations across 11 ISAM functional areas. Inclusion does not imply flight qualification or business viability."}, {"id": "sp14", "title": "NASA: OSAM-1 cancellation announcement", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/mission/on-orbit-servicing-assembly-and-manufacturing-1/", "claim": "NASA discontinued OSAM-1 after cost, schedule, technical and transition-partner challenges. A useful warning against treating every frontier program as durable employment."}, {"id": "sp15", "title": "NASA: Life-support subsystems", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/reference/jsc-life-support-subsystems/", "claim": "NASA develops and tests open- and closed-loop life-support technologies for long-duration presence."}, {"id": "sp16", "title": "NASA: ISS water-recovery milestone", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/nasa-achieves-water-recovery-milestone-on-international-space-station/", "claim": "NASA reported a 98% total water-recovery demonstration in 2023, not a fully self-sufficient ecosystem."}, {"id": "sp17", "title": "Collins Aerospace: Crewed missions", "url": "https://www.rtx.com/collinsaerospace/what-we-do/industries/space/crewed-missions", "claim": "Collins supplies environmental control/life support and other human-spaceflight systems."}, {"id": "sp18", "title": "NASA: Commercial space stations", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/commercial-space/commercial-space-stations/", "claim": "NASA supports development of commercial LEO destinations; this establishes a development ecosystem, not guaranteed future stations or staffing."}, {"id": "sp19", "title": "GAO: ISS-to-commercial-station transition, June 2026", "url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-26-107805", "claim": "GAO identifies commercial-station schedule risks and possible gaps in human presence; as of May 2026 the acquisition approach remained unsettled."}, {"id": "sp20", "title": "Vast: Haven-1 integration update", "url": "https://www.vastspace.com/updates/vast-advances-haven-1-into-integration-phase", "claim": "Vast describes integration of station thermal control, life support and propulsion fluid systems; integration is a development milestone."}, {"id": "sp21", "title": "Sierra Space: LIFE habitat", "url": "https://www.sierraspace.com/commercial-space-stations/life-space-habitat/", "claim": "LIFE has undergone ground structural testing; successful burst tests are not the same as a complete crewed station operating in orbit."}, {"id": "sp22", "title": "Axiom Space: AxEMU suit", "url": "https://axiomspace.com/axiom-suit", "claim": "Axiom is developing an EVA spacesuit for lunar exploration."}, {"id": "sp23", "title": "NASA: Human factors and performance", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/reference/jsc-human-factors-performance/", "claim": "Human-systems integration accounts for human capabilities/limitations and includes crew interfaces, habitability and human-in-the-loop evaluation."}, {"id": "sp24", "title": "NASA: Human systems engineering", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/esdmd/hhp/human-systems-engineering/", "claim": "Human-spaceflight engineering spans requirements, interfaces, verification, flight certification, risk, medical operations, radiation, acoustics and other disciplines."}, {"id": "sp25", "title": "NASA: Planetary construction technology", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/stmd/nasa-enables-construction-technology-for-moon-and-mars-exploration/", "claim": "NASA/ICON work explores autonomous construction with local materials; Earth analog habitats and demonstrations do not establish lunar building operations."}, {"id": "sp26", "title": "NASA TechPort: MMPACT project", "url": "https://techport.nasa.gov/projects/116319", "claim": "MMPACT's entry lists completed technology-project status, with regolith-processing and prototype construction work; its end does not establish an ongoing lunar construction service."}, {"id": "sp27", "title": "Blue Origin: Blue Alchemist milestone", "url": "https://www.blueorigin.com/news/blue-alchemist-hits-major-milestone-toward-permanent-sustainable-lunar-infrastructure", "claim": "Blue Alchemist develops processing of regolith into oxygen, metals and solar-power materials; operational lunar industry remains a future objective."}, {"id": "sp28", "title": "NASA: Moon Base rover progress, October 2026", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/missions/moon-base/nasa-provides-progress-update-on-moon-base-rovers/", "claim": "NASA describes Astrolab and Lunar Outpost task orders and planned Blue Origin delivery; awards and targets do not prove future service deployment."}, {"id": "sp29", "title": "Varda: W-1 mission", "url": "https://www.varda.com/mission/w-1", "claim": "W-1 returned in February 2024 after a pharmaceutical-crystallization demonstration."}, {"id": "sp30", "title": "Redwire: Research and manufacturing in microgravity", "url": "https://rdw.com/capabilities/research-and-manufacturing/", "claim": "Redwire develops/operates ISS research and manufacturing hardware, including pharmaceutical and bioprinting experiments. This does not establish routine organ manufacturing."}, {"id": "sp31", "title": "NASA: Jessica Watkins biography", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/astronauts/jessica-watkins/", "claim": "Watkins trained in geology, demonstrating that astronaut crews are not exclusively aerospace engineers."}, {"id": "co-taxonomy", "title": "NASA 2024 Technology Taxonomy", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/otps/2024-nasa-technology-taxonomy/", "claim": "NASA organizes aerospace technology into 17 areas, including propulsion, flight computing, power, robotics, thermal management, flight vehicles, and GNC. This is a useful map of disciplines, not a list of degree titles or employment forecasts."}, {"id": "co-gnc", "title": "NASA 2026 Small Spacecraft report: Guidance, Navigation, and Control", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/guidance-navigation-and-control/", "claim": "Surveys attitude determination and control, sensors, actuators, and rendezvous/proximity operations; the 2026 edition updates the latter. These are established engineering functions with continuing development."}, {"id": "co-draper", "title": "Draper: Space Navigation and ISAM", "url": "https://www.draper.com/market-areas/space-systems/space-navigation-and-isam", "claim": "Draper develops vision-based navigation, inertial sensing, GNC, autonomy, and perception for space navigation and servicing."}, {"id": "co-startrackers", "title": "Rocket Lab: Star Trackers", "url": "https://rocketlabcorp.com/space-systems/satellite-components/star-trackers/", "claim": "Rocket Lab offers flight-proven and higher-performance star trackers, including radiation-hardening and manufacturing considerations."}, {"id": "co-hpsc", "title": "NASA TechPort: High-Performance Spaceflight Computing", "url": "https://techport.nasa.gov/projects/13569", "claim": "NASA's August 2026 project record describes HPSC as completed technology development addressing computation, power management, and fault tolerance for missions through 2040 and beyond. A completed development project does not mean every future mission has adopted the chip."}, {"id": "co-microchip", "title": "Microchip: PIC64-HPSC program milestones, October 2026", "url": "https://www.microchip.com/en-us/about/news-releases/products/microchip-achieves-key-program-milestones-for-pic64-hpsc-spaceflight-computing-series", "claim": "Microchip reports milestones for a multicore RISC-V platform designed for spacecraft and other mission-critical space systems."}, {"id": "co-bae", "title": "BAE Systems: Space Electronics", "url": "https://www.baesystems.com/en-us/who-we-are/electronic-systems/c4isr/space-electronics", "claim": "BAE supplies radiation-hardened computers, ASIC technologies, packaging, and radiation-assurance services for space applications."}, {"id": "co-power", "title": "NASA 2026 Small Spacecraft report: Power", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/power-subsystems/", "claim": "Electrical power spans generation, storage, and distribution. Mission-specific constraints include mass, volume, radiation, thermal environment, eclipse, and end-of-life degradation."}, {"id": "co-solar", "title": "Rocket Lab: Space Solar Solutions", "url": "https://rocketlabcorp.com/space-systems/solar/", "claim": "Rocket Lab supplies space solar cells, panels, substrates, and arrays, with manufacturing, environmental testing, and semiconductor R&D capabilities."}, {"id": "co-redwire-power", "title": "Redwire: Power products", "url": "https://rdw.com/product-archive/power/", "claim": "Redwire's power portfolio includes deployable solar-array technology; its current site reports delivered ISS roll-out solar array wings."}, {"id": "co-thermal", "title": "NASA 2026 Small Spacecraft report: Thermal Control", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/thermal-control/", "claim": "Thermal design balances mass, radiating area, volume, and power. Passive coatings, insulation, and thermal straps remain common; active cooling and deployable radiators continue to develop."}, {"id": "co-loxsat", "title": "NASA: Cryogenic Fuel Technology Demonstration, May 2026", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/stmd/tech-demo-missions-program/cryogenic-fluid-management-cfm/nasa-industry-prepare-cryogenic-fuel-technology-demo/", "claim": "LOXSAT is a demonstration intended to mature storage and transfer of cryogenic propellant in orbit, with Eta Space and Rocket Lab. A demonstration plan is not proof of an operational depot network."}, {"id": "co-eta", "title": "Eta Space: Program news", "url": "https://etaspace.com/news", "claim": "Eta Space reports work on LOXSAT, cryogenic cooling, and liquid hydrogen systems. Its current work illustrates a supplier-sized route into space infrastructure."}, {"id": "co-act", "title": "Advanced Cooling Technologies: 2026 Space brochure", "url": "https://www.1-act.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/SPC-BRO_Space-Brochure_V2_2026-02-13_Web-1.pdf", "claim": "ACT provides thermal design, manufacture, and qualification for spacecraft, launch vehicles, and scientific payloads."}, {"id": "co-propulsion", "title": "NASA 2026 Small Spacecraft report: In-Space Propulsion", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/in-space_propulsion/", "claim": "Chemical propulsion suits high thrust and rapid maneuvers; electric systems trade low thrust and electrical power demand for efficient propellant use. Flight maturity varies by device, and vendor claims require verification."}, {"id": "co-blue-engines", "title": "Blue Origin: Engines", "url": "https://www.blueorigin.com/engines", "claim": "Blue Origin develops engines for launch, upper stages, and lunar landing, including BE-4, BE-3U, and BE-7."}, {"id": "co-rocketdyne", "title": "Rocketdyne: Propulsion and Power Systems", "url": "https://rocketdyne.com/", "claim": "Rocketdyne's current site presents space propulsion, electronics, and power work, and notes the 2026 announcement of the sale of a majority stake in the former L3Harris space propulsion/power business."}, {"id": "co-safran", "title": "Safran Spacecraft Propulsion", "url": "https://www.safran-group.com/companies/safran-spacecraft-propulsion", "claim": "Safran offers Hall-effect thrusters and complete propulsion subsystems, including fluid control and power processing."}, {"id": "co-exotrail", "title": "Exotrail: Electric propulsion technology", "url": "https://www.exotrail.com/our-technology", "claim": "Exotrail develops miniaturized Hall-effect electric propulsion for small spacecraft."}, {"id": "co-nuclear", "title": "NASA: SR-1 Freedom nuclear power module progress, October 20, 2026", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/sr-1-freedom-advances-with-progress-on-nuclear-power-module/", "claim": "NASA reports Sierra Lobo power-conversion work and an Idaho National Laboratory reactor for the planned SR-1 nuclear-electric demonstration, targeting late 2028. This remains a development plan as of the research date."}, {"id": "co-ntp", "title": "NASA: Space Nuclear Propulsion", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/space-technology-mission-directorate/tdm/space-nuclear-propulsion/", "claim": "NASA describes nuclear thermal and nuclear electric concepts and reports 2025 nuclear-thermal contract extensions to General Atomics and Standard Nuclear. These are not interchangeable with radioisotope power."}, {"id": "co-edl", "title": "NASA: Entry, Descent, and Landing", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/entry-descent-and-landing/", "claim": "NASA's EDL work includes mission design, atmospheric simulation, ground/flight testing, aerobraking, aerocapture, and landing systems."}, {"id": "co-esm", "title": "NASA: Entry Systems Modeling", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/entry-systems-modeling-esm-2/", "claim": "Entry-system research combines predictive models and validation experiments for heat shields, aerothermal environments, and the coupled landing system."}, {"id": "co-loftid", "title": "NASA: LOFTID", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/mission/low-earth-orbit-flight-test-of-an-inflatable-decelerator-loftid/", "claim": "LOFTID successfully demonstrated an inflatable atmospheric-entry aeroshell on November 10, 2022. This was a flight demonstration, not a crew-rated Mars landing system."}, {"id": "co-varda", "title": "Varda: W-5 mission", "url": "https://www.varda.com/mission/w-5", "claim": "Varda describes a recoverable reentry capsule and an internally manufactured C-PICA heat shield, illustrating commercial reentry work."}, {"id": "co-structures", "title": "NASA 2026 Small Spacecraft report: Structures, Materials, and Mechanisms", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/structures-materials-and-mechanisms/", "claim": "Space structures span primary load paths, deployables, and mechanisms. Compact packaging and reliable deployment remain active constraints."}, {"id": "co-rampt", "title": "NASA: Rapid Analysis and Manufacturing Propulsion Technology", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/rapid-analysis-and-manufacturing-propulsion-technology/", "claim": "RAMPT matured additive manufacturing, alloys, and composite approaches for rocket components and reports industrial transfer and testing."}, {"id": "co-ngstructures", "title": "Northrop Grumman: Space Structures", "url": "https://www.northropgrumman.com/what-we-do/space/mission-enabling-products/structures", "claim": "Northrop Grumman provides composite spacecraft components and deployable support structures, emphasizing thermal stability and deployment accuracy."}, {"id": "co-rocketlab", "title": "Rocket Lab: About Us", "url": "https://rocketlabcorp.com/about/about-us/", "claim": "Rocket Lab's current capability portfolio includes spacecraft, composite structures, flight software, solar power, star trackers, reaction wheels, and radios."}, {"id": "co-dsoc", "title": "NASA: Deep Space Optical Communications", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/mission/deep-space-optical-communications-dsoc/", "claim": "DSOC completed a deep-space optical communication technology demonstration in 2025. It establishes a demonstrated capability, not a completed solar-system internet."}, {"id": "co-mynaric", "title": "Mynaric: CONDOR Mk3", "url": "https://mynaric.com/products/space/condor-mk3/", "claim": "Mynaric markets an optical communication terminal for space applications."}, {"id": "co-mynaric-acq", "title": "Rocket Lab SEC filing: Mynaric acquisition completed, April 14, 2026", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1819994/000175392626000654/g085683_8k.htm", "claim": "Rocket Lab reported completion of the Mynaric acquisition on April 14, 2026. The two names should not be presented as fully independent employer bets."}, {"id": "co-newglenn", "title": "Blue Origin: New Glenn", "url": "https://www.blueorigin.com/new-glenn", "claim": "New Glenn includes aerodynamic control surfaces and strakes for the reusable first stage's descent, illustrating a commercial aerodynamics/reentry application."}, {"id": "co-redwire-rf", "title": "Redwire: RF Systems and Satellite Payloads", "url": "https://rdw.com/capabilities/rf-systems-and-satellite-payloads/", "claim": "Redwire develops RF systems and satellite payload hardware, illustrating a supplier path alongside large satellite primes."}, {"id": "co-systems", "title": "NASA Systems Engineering Handbook: Fundamentals", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/reference/2-0-fundamentals-of-systems-engineering/", "claim": "Systems engineering spans requirements, architecture, interfaces, trades, operations, and technical risk across the system life cycle. It is a substantive technical discipline, not simply being unspecialized."}, {"id": "co-verify", "title": "NASA: Verification versus Validation", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/reference/2-4-distinctions-between-product-verification-and-product-validation/", "claim": "Verification establishes compliance with requirements; validation establishes that the system fulfills its intended purpose in its intended environment."}, {"id": "co-test", "title": "ESA: Building and Testing Spacecraft", "url": "https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Building_and_testing_spacecraft", "claim": "Spacecraft development includes integrated models and environmental testing such as thermal vacuum, vibration, and acoustics."}, {"id": "co-redwire", "title": "Redwire: Current Capabilities and Missions", "url": "https://rdw.com/", "claim": "Redwire lists spacecraft integration and test, avionics, power, RF hardware, and structures, with current mission examples including PROBA-3."}, {"id": "op-bls-ae", "title": "BLS: Aerospace Engineers (May 2025 pay; 2025–35 outlook)", "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/aerospace-engineers.htm", "claim": "U.S. median annual wage $134,960 in May 2025; projected occupation growth 8% in 2025–35; related engineering degrees can enter; some defense jobs require a clearance."}, {"id": "op-bls-ee", "title": "BLS: Electrical and Electronics Engineers", "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/electrical-and-electronics-engineers.htm", "claim": "May 2025 U.S. medians: electrical engineers $120,630; electronics engineers except computer $130,220. The page reports different wages by industry."}, {"id": "op-bls-me", "title": "BLS: Mechanical Engineers", "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/mechanical-engineers.htm", "claim": "May 2025 U.S. median $104,110; projected employment growth 11% in 2025–35. Mechanical engineering includes robots, thermal devices, design, testing and manufacturing."}, {"id": "op-bls-manager", "title": "BLS: Architectural and Engineering Managers", "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/architectural-and-engineering-managers.htm", "claim": "May 2025 U.S. median $171,270. The occupation typically requires considerable prior engineering experience and coordinates people, budgets and technical work."}, {"id": "op-abet", "title": "ABET: Engineering Accreditation Criteria, 2026–2027", "url": "https://www.abet.org/accreditation/accreditation-criteria/criteria-for-accrediting-engineering-programs-2026-2027/", "claim": "Aerospace programs cover atmospheric and space flight plus modeling, simulation, computing and testing. ME concerns physical systems and EE concerns electrical/electronic hardware and software systems."}, {"id": "op-ucsd-ae", "title": "UC San Diego: Aerospace Engineering Specializations", "url": "https://mae.ucsd.edu/undergrad/aero-eng-specializations", "claim": "Current optional AE specializations: Astrodynamics and Space Applications, Aerothermodynamics, Flight Dynamics and Controls. Students are encouraged to plan at least two years before graduation because offerings vary."}, {"id": "op-ucsd-mae", "title": "UC San Diego: Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering undergraduate catalog", "url": "https://catalog.ucsd.edu/curric/MAE-ug.html", "claim": "ME Controls and Robotics includes embedded control, robotic planning, autonomous vehicles, controls and robotics electives; AE Flight Dynamics and Controls includes several overlapping electives."}, {"id": "op-whoi", "title": "WHOI: Oceanographic Systems Lab", "url": "https://www2.whoi.edu/site/osl/", "claim": "The laboratory develops autonomous underwater vehicles through mechanical and electronic design; it is a research-employer example, not a forecast of future vacancies."}, {"id": "op-remus", "title": "WHOI: REMUS underwater vehicles", "url": "https://www.whoi.edu/what-we-do/explore/underwater-vehicles/auvs/remus/", "claim": "REMUS vehicles support scientific and civil applications including aqueduct inspection as well as ocean exploration."}, {"id": "op-hii", "title": "HII: Unmanned Systems", "url": "https://www.hii.com/capabilities/unmanned-systems", "claim": "HII markets REMUS underwater vehicles and autonomy products across maritime missions."}, {"id": "op-kongsberg", "title": "Kongsberg: HUGIN autonomous underwater vehicles", "url": "https://www.kongsberg.com/discovery/autonomous-and-uncrewed-solutions/auv/hugin/", "claim": "HUGIN is a commercial autonomous underwater vehicle family with a history in subsea survey."}, {"id": "op-anduril", "title": "Anduril: Dive-LD", "url": "https://www.anduril.com/dive-ld", "claim": "Anduril is an example of a company developing large autonomous underwater vehicles."}, {"id": "op-gao26", "title": "GAO: Weapon Systems Annual Assessment 2026", "url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-26-108457", "claim": "July 2026 assessment describes persistent schedule, technology-maturity and delivery problems across major defense programs, supporting continued need for development, integration and test rather than a claim that weapons engineering is finished."}, {"id": "op-gao25", "title": "GAO: Weapon Systems Annual Assessment 2025", "url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-25-107569", "claim": "GAO describes increasingly complex, software-driven weapon systems and acquisition problems; this supports high-level career discussion, not technical weapon design."}, {"id": "op-lm-careers", "title": "Lockheed Martin: Missiles and Fire Control career areas", "url": "https://www.lockheedmartin.com/en-us/careers/why-lm/business-areas/mfc.html", "claim": "Lists electrical, mechanical, GNC, RF, embedded software, systems, integration/test, sustainment and manufacturing specialties as current career areas."}, {"id": "op-rtx", "title": "RTX: Careers", "url": "https://careers.rtx.com/global/en", "claim": "RTX's businesses span aviation and defense, including Collins Aerospace, Pratt & Whitney and Raytheon."}, {"id": "op-ng", "title": "Northrop Grumman: Careers", "url": "https://jobs.northropgrumman.com/careers", "claim": "Current job families include systems/architecture/test, mechanical and electronics engineering; listings are examples, not promised 2034 openings."}, {"id": "op-atlas", "title": "Boston Dynamics: Atlas", "url": "https://bostondynamics.com/products/atlas/", "claim": "Atlas is being developed and introduced for industrial work, with customer field testing and applications such as sequencing and machine tending. Earth operation does not establish space qualification."}, {"id": "op-valkyrie", "title": "NASA: Valkyrie (R5)", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/technology/r5/", "claim": "NASA's humanoid research draws together electronics, actuators and sensing, illustrating multidisciplinary robotics rather than an aerospace-only degree path."}, {"id": "op-intuitive", "title": "Intuitive: da Vinci robotic surgical systems", "url": "https://www.intuitive.com/en-us/products-and-services/da-vinci", "claim": "Surgical robotics offers a terrestrial domain for precision mechanisms, vision, control and human-machine interfaces."}, {"id": "op-ekso", "title": "Ekso Bionics: EksoNR", "url": "https://eksobionics.com/en/eksonr/", "claim": "EksoNR is a powered robotic exoskeleton for clinical rehabilitation, a real example of wearable robotics rather than an armored flying suit."}, {"id": "op-esa-debris", "title": "ESA: Space Environment Report 2026", "url": "https://www.esa.int/Space_Safety/Space_Debris/ESA_Space_Environment_Report_2026", "claim": "September 2026 report based on data through 2025 describes growing constellations and debris pressures; current environment is evidence, future market size is uncertain."}, {"id": "op-tracss", "title": "Office of Space Commerce: TraCSS", "url": "https://space.commerce.gov/traffic-coordination-system-for-space-tracss/", "claim": "TraCSS provides civil space traffic safety data and services; it began accepting owner/operator registrations in early 2026."}, {"id": "op-astroscale", "title": "Astroscale: ADRAS-J completed operations and began deorbit", "url": "https://www.astroscale.com/en/news/astroscales-adras-j-mission-completes-operations-begins-deorbit", "claim": "ADRAS-J completed inspection operations in 2026; follow-on ADRAS-J2 is intended to remove the target. Inspection and actual target removal are different milestones."}, {"id": "op-leolabs", "title": "LeoLabs: Space Traffic Management", "url": "https://leolabs.space/space-traffic-management/", "claim": "LeoLabs supplies commercial tracking and conjunction-alert services; avoid treating vendor performance superlatives as independent validation."}, {"id": "op-slingshot", "title": "Slingshot Aerospace: government and defense solutions", "url": "https://www.slingshot.space/solutions-government-and-defense", "claim": "Slingshot offers sensor networks, orbit determination, conjunction assessment and space-domain intelligence."}, {"id": "op-noaa-weather", "title": "NOAA: Space Weather Impacts", "url": "https://www.spaceweather.gov/impacts", "claim": "Solar energetic particles can damage satellite electronics; space weather also affects radio communications and other infrastructure."}, {"id": "op-vigil", "title": "ESA: Vigil mission overview", "url": "https://www.esa.int/Space_Safety/Vigil/Vigil_mission_overview", "claim": "Vigil is a planned space-weather mission, currently targeting launch in 2031; Airbus is the prime contractor. A planned date is not a guaranteed milestone."}, {"id": "op-ral", "title": "RAL Space: Vigil", "url": "https://www.ralspace.stfc.ac.uk/mission/vigil", "claim": "RAL Space develops Vigil remote-sensing instrumentation, illustrating scientific instrument engineering roles."}, {"id": "op-jpl-protection", "title": "JPL: Europa Clipper biological cleanliness", "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/press-kits/europa-clipper/mission/biological-cleanliness/", "claim": "Planetary protection limits biological contamination; contamination control also manages nonliving material that could impair spacecraft instruments."}, {"id": "op-nasa-protection", "title": "NASA: Planetary Protection discipline", "url": "https://sma.nasa.gov/sma-disciplines/planetary-protection", "claim": "NASA describes planetary protection as mission-dependent contamination control and says careers require a strong background spanning areas such as engineering and microbiology."}, {"id": "op-jpl-team", "title": "JPL: Spacecraft Makers—keeping Europa Clipper clean", "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/videos/spacecraft-makers-how-we-keep-europa-clipper-super-clean/", "claim": "Shows planetary protection specialists and an engineering lead doing real spacecraft work, distinguishing the occupation from purely speculative astrobiology."}, {"id": "ex-mechanisms-nasa", "title": "NASA: Small spacecraft structures, materials and mechanisms", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/structures-materials-and-mechanisms/", "claim": "Current technology survey distinguishes deployment, release, pointing, gimbals and actuation, and identifies reliability, compact packaging, mass and power constraints."}, {"id": "ex-tribology-esa", "title": "ESA: How to make moves in space", "url": "https://www.esa.int/Enabling_Support/Space_Engineering_Technology/Talking_technology/How_to_make_moves_in_space", "claim": "ESA mechanism engineers develop moving spacecraft hardware, including pointing and deployment mechanisms; wear and lubrication over long unmaintained missions are central concerns."}, {"id": "ex-moog", "title": "Moog: Space Mechanisms", "url": "https://www.moog.com/products/space-mechanisms.html", "claim": "Moog offers spacecraft gimbals, linear and rotary actuators, solar-array drives and optical filter wheels, with integrated engineering, manufacturing and test capability."}, {"id": "ex-rise", "title": "CFM International: RISE program", "url": "https://www.cfmaeroengines.com/rise", "claim": "CFM describes a technology-development program that could support a next engine generation in the second half of the 2030s; its efficiency and availability statements are program goals, not guaranteed delivered performance."}, {"id": "ex-epfd", "title": "NASA: Electrified Powertrain Flight Demonstration", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/armd/iasp/epfd/", "claim": "NASA records work with GE Aerospace and magniX on hybrid-electric propulsion tests. The page explicitly says EPFD concluded and was archived as of August 14, 2026; it must not be presented as an ongoing future employment program."}, {"id": "ex-faa", "title": "FAA: AC 21.17-4, Type Certification—Powered-lift", "url": "https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/advisory_circulars/index.cfm/go/document.information/documentID/1044836", "claim": "Active guidance issued July 18, 2025 addresses type, production and airworthiness certification for powered-lift; certification is a distinct engineering requirement, not implied by a successful prototype flight."}], "spaceSections": {"centralRecommendation": {"title": "Build the mechanisms and robots that make space infrastructure work.", "text": "Your current aerospace/ME direction and interest in mechanisms, motion, assembly and physical hardware point first toward spacecraft mechanisms and robotic hardware, supported by dynamics, controls and enough embedded integration to make the machine work. Orbital servicing and assembly give that combination a mission. A future branch could be dexterous robots, capture mechanisms, lunar mobility or habitat maintenance. This keeps the physical engineering you enjoy at the center while preserving options if individual space programs slip.", "interpretation": "Personal fit judgment and scenario-based inference, not a measured ranking of future salaries or hiring.", "sourceIds": ["sp06", "sp07", "sp10", "sp11"]}, "humanoids": {"title": "The space robot might not look human.", "text": "A humanoid makes sense when the job depends on tools, access paths and workstations built for human bodies. A free-flying inspection cube, a rail-mounted arm, a wheeled rover or a purpose-built mechanism can be simpler for other jobs. The specialty to pursue is dexterous manipulation, locomotion, perception, actuation or human-robot coordination—not a commitment that every future machine must have two legs. NASA-supported Apollo and Valkyrie work gives this interest a real industrial bridge; it does not prove humanoids will dominate lunar operations.", "sourceIds": ["sp06", "sp09", "sp10"]}, "flying": {"title": "Building for space and working in space are different career tracks.", "text": "Most spacecraft are designed, integrated, tested and operated by people on Earth. An aerospace degree can qualify you for engineering work and can be one part of an astronaut application. It is not a ticket to orbit. The relevant question is what unique mission capability you would bring as a crewmember, and whether that mission needs a human there.", "currentEligibility": "NASA currently lists U.S. citizenship, a qualifying STEM master's or accepted equivalent, three years of related professional experience after degree completion or qualifying pilot experience, and ability to pass its long-duration astronaut physical. Leadership, teamwork and communication matter. Future selection calls can change the details; your citizenship and medical eligibility have not been assumed.", "selection": "NASA's 2025 class selected 10 candidates from more than 8,000 applications. This illustrates rarity, not your personal probability. Selection leads to training and eligibility for assignments, not an immediate flight.", "degree": "NASA explicitly declines to recommend one qualifying degree over another. Engineers, physicians, scientists and pilots all contribute. A geological scientist may be especially useful on a field-science mission; a roboticist may be useful on a construction mission. Neither is a guaranteed selection preference.", "timing": "If 2033 means your bachelor's completion, 2034 is a reasonable target for an entry engineering role, not an assumed astronaut start date. A master's and a record of professional excellence may move a credible application window later into the 2030s or 2040s. Treat this as a second ambition alongside a career you would still want if you never fly.", "sourceIds": ["sp01", "sp02", "sp03", "sp31"]}, "spaceflightPaths": [{"name": "Agency astronaut: engineer and operator", "status": "Established route; exceptionally selective", "path": "STEM foundation → excellent professional/research record → qualifying graduate education/experience → apply in actual selection cycles → training → possible mission assignment.", "specialties": "Vehicle systems, flight testing, robotics operations, ECLSS, electrical or mechanical engineering can all contribute. Teamwork and operational judgment supplement technical depth.", "tradeoff": "A very narrow specialist still has to function as a broad, adaptable crew member. No specialty has a validated best selection rate.", "sourceIds": ["sp01", "sp02", "sp03"]}, {"name": "Commercial flight operations and test specialist", "status": "Demonstrated examples; no predictable employment pipeline", "path": "Build responsibility in crew systems, training, mission control or flight-test operations at a company conducting human spaceflight.", "specialties": "Crew operations, spacecraft systems integration, mission control, biomedical operations, EVA development and training.", "tradeoff": "Gillis and Menon flew after operational engineering work. Their flights show possibility, not a promise that employees of a space company will fly.", "sourceIds": ["sp04", "sp05"]}, {"name": "Payload or research specialist", "status": "Established mission-specific concept; future demand uncertain", "path": "Become responsible for a funded experiment or capability whose sponsor can justify a crewed mission role.", "specialties": "Microgravity process science, medicine, biology, materials, field geology and experiment engineering.", "tradeoff": "Many payloads run remotely or can be operated by existing crew. Deep expertise may send your experiment to space while you stay on Earth.", "sourceIds": ["sp03", "sp30", "sp31"]}, {"name": "Future station maintenance or lunar field engineer", "status": "Plausible 2030s–2040s scenario, not a mature job category", "path": "Develop reliability, diagnosis, repair, field operations and cross-system competence; watch for actual hiring and funded missions rather than inferred job titles.", "specialties": "Life support, power/thermal, mechatronics, EVA operations, instrument repair, construction robotics and habitat operations.", "tradeoff": "Robotics, logistics economics, station utilization and safety may keep the number of humans small. No defensible ranking currently says these specialties will earn the most seats.", "sourceIds": ["sp06", "sp19", "sp23"]}], "scenarios": [{"name": "Slow expansion", "period": "2034–2040", "description": "Launches and satellites keep creating engineering work, while station/lunar programs remain small, delayed or concentrated in a few contractors.", "strategy": "Choose transferable controls, embedded systems, thermal, power, reliability and manufacturing capabilities. Keep a space portfolio while building credible industrial experience.", "evidence": "Current station-transition risk and historical program cancellation justify planning for this case.", "sourceIds": ["sp14", "sp19"]}, {"name": "Infrastructure grows", "period": "2034–2040", "description": "Commercial stations, lunar logistics and servicing operate often enough to need sustained maintenance, testing, autonomy and upgrades.", "strategy": "Specialize in a persistent bottleneck: contact dynamics, robotic manipulation, fault diagnosis, closed-loop resource systems, docking interfaces, dust-tolerant mechanisms or human-system verification.", "evidence": "These capabilities have current prototypes, demonstrations or development programs; market scale is a forecast.", "sourceIds": ["sp06", "sp11", "sp13", "sp18", "sp28"]}, {"name": "Industrial space accelerates", "period": "2040s", "description": "Multiple orbital facilities or sustained surface activity could create larger demand for construction, local resource processing, industrial autonomy and lifecycle engineering.", "strategy": "Be ready to transfer an established technical specialty into the new market. Do not require a Mars city or huge lunar workforce to justify your degree.", "evidence": "An ambitious scenario, not a baseline prediction; economics, funding and transport capacity remain gating factors.", "sourceIds": ["sp25", "sp27", "sp29", "sp30"]}], "highValueCombinations": [{"name": "Roboticist who understands spacecraft", "combination": "Controls + estimation + manipulation + flight software + vacuum/radiation constraints", "why": "Connects autonomy demonstrations to a vehicle that must actually work.", "maturity": "Strong transferable foundation"}, {"name": "Life-support engineer who understands autonomy", "combination": "Thermofluids + chemistry + sensors + fault detection + maintainability", "why": "A habitat must sustain people and diagnose failures when expert help is distant.", "maturity": "Established need; frontier scaling"}, {"name": "Robotic construction engineer who understands soil", "combination": "Mechatronics + terramechanics + materials processing + power budgeting", "why": "A robot can navigate perfectly and still fail because its wheel sinks or its excavation forces exceed traction.", "maturity": "Terrestrial base; lunar market uncertain"}, {"name": "Human-spaceflight engineer who can operate the system", "combination": "Hardware depth + test leadership + mission procedures + crew interfaces", "why": "Useful on the ground and relevant to rare flight roles, without depending on selection.", "maturity": "Established ground work; flight opportunity scarce"}, {"name": "In-space manufacturing engineer who understands customers", "combination": "Process control + payload automation + quality measurement + return logistics", "why": "A beautiful orbital experiment becomes an industry only if the product justifies the entire delivery chain.", "maturity": "Demonstrated experiments; selective commercialization"}]}, "optionSections": [{"id": "degree-route", "title": "Aerospace can be your home base; robotics is a cross-department craft.", "paragraphs": ["Your aerospace-first direction makes sense for vehicles, flight, mechanisms and the ambition to build places humans can reach and occupy. Choose the degree whose actual laboratories, design teams and electives let you build those capabilities. A degree title alone does not decide which subsystem you will work on.", "At UC San Diego today, AE offers Astrodynamics and Space Applications, Aerothermodynamics, and Flight Dynamics and Controls. The controls option includes embedded robotics, planning and estimation choices. These are useful launch points; your later professional niche could be much narrower than the words on the diploma.", "ME remains a serious alternative if gears, actuators, fasteners, joints, fabrication and robot bodies become the central fascination. Its Controls and Robotics option can lead toward space mechanisms and autonomous machines. EE becomes especially relevant if you discover that motor drives, sensing, avionics or power hardware are the part you most want to own. It need not replace your aerospace preference.", "Aerospace specialization is not an irreversible choice between space, airplanes and the ocean. Dynamics, estimation, mechanisms, thermal design, manufacturing and verification transfer. You will still need to learn the new domain: orbital motion differs from underwater navigation, and vacuum hardware differs from pressure-resistant subsea hardware."], "sourceIds": ["op-ucsd-ae", "op-ucsd-mae", "op-abet", "op-bls-ae"]}, {"id": "specialist-pay", "title": "Depth pays when somebody urgently needs the depth.", "paragraphs": ["There is no general rule that the narrowest specialist earns the most, or that space pays less than weapons. Compensation follows the employer, location, seniority, responsibility, scarce skills, available budgets and the total offer. A tiny niche can have very few employers; broad system responsibility can be highly valuable.", "The robust strategy is a broad engineering foundation, one capability you can demonstrate deeply, and the ability to integrate it with neighboring systems. For you that might become aerospace + robotic mechanisms + controls + repeated hardware testing. It is a stronger bet than selecting a science-fiction job title now.", "Current U.S. wage medians describe entire occupations across experience levels. They are neither starting salaries nor estimates of your pay in 2034. They also do not isolate commercial space from defense, and they cannot tell us whether specializing caused someone to earn more. The engineering-manager figure illustrates that coordination and broad responsibility are not automatically low-value; it is not a like-for-like comparison with new graduates."], "sourceIds": ["op-bls-ae", "op-bls-ee", "op-bls-me", "op-bls-manager"], "payData": {"year": 2025, "geography": "United States", "measure": "Median annual wage, May 2025; all experience levels", "rows": [{"occupation": "Aerospace engineers", "median": 134960, "sourceId": "op-bls-ae"}, {"occupation": "Electrical engineers", "median": 120630, "sourceId": "op-bls-ee"}, {"occupation": "Electronics engineers, except computer", "median": 130220, "sourceId": "op-bls-ee"}, {"occupation": "Mechanical engineers", "median": 104110, "sourceId": "op-bls-me"}, {"occupation": "Architectural and engineering managers", "median": 171270, "sourceId": "op-bls-manager"}]}}, {"id": "defense-future", "title": "Defense is unlikely to run out of engineering problems.", "paragraphs": ["The problem is not simply inventing another shape of missile. Hardware must remain reliable, producible, maintainable and affordable while electronics, software, operating environments and requirements change. GAO's 2026 assessment still finds major maturity and delivery problems. That is evidence of unfinished engineering, not proof of any particular 2040 weapons program.", "If defense becomes your route, choose a technical craft: structures, thermal engineering, control, electronics, manufacturing or systems test. Those identities can carry across sectors. Some positions require clearance or citizenship eligibility, and classified work can limit what you can publish in a portfolio. Check the actual role rather than assuming every aerospace or defense job has identical restrictions.", "Space and defense also overlap through launch, satellites, sensing, communications and resilient infrastructure. You can prefer civilian habitation and robotics without needing a prediction that defense will become redundant. Your interest is enough reason to prioritize space."], "sourceIds": ["op-gao26", "op-gao25", "op-lm-careers", "op-bls-ae"]}, {"id": "iron-man", "title": "The useful translation of Iron Man is several engineering teams.", "paragraphs": ["A responsive powered suit translates into wearable robotics, biomechanics, compact actuation, batteries, heat removal, embedded controls and human factors. Dexterous robots add manipulation, perception and reliable contact with the world. Flight adds another demanding stack of propulsion, aerodynamics, structures and safety. There is no single established Iron Man specialization, and today's exoskeletons do not imply a practical armored flying suit.", "This is a reason to get specific about which experience excites you: the mechanism moving, the human-machine interface, the flying vehicle, or the autonomy. Boston Dynamics, Ekso Bionics, Intuitive and NASA's humanoid research provide real examples of parts of that picture. The transferable work may be exciting even when the final product is a factory robot, rehabilitation device or orbital maintenance arm.", "A humanoid shape is useful when a task and environment reward it; it is not automatically the best space robot. A rail-mounted arm, rover or purpose-built inspection mechanism may be simpler. Learn to choose a form from the mission, then make it work."], "sourceIds": ["op-atlas", "op-ekso", "op-intuitive", "op-valkyrie"]}, {"id": "timing", "title": "Choose a capability by 2031, then let the missions compete for it.", "paragraphs": ["For a tentative 2033 graduation, use the early years to build mathematics, physics, programming and practical fabrication habits. By roughly 2030–31, compare two or three specialties through classes, projects, research and internships. UCSD currently recommends planning AE specialization coursework at least two years before graduation; your eventual catalog and transfer plan must be rechecked.", "By 2032–33, aim to own one subsystem on a substantial team project and explain its requirements, calculations, failures and measured results. A focused master's degree can deepen controls, robotics, thermal systems or research-intensive niches, but it is a choice tied to a target role, not a universal requirement for starting an engineering career.", "For 2034–40, watch actual flight demonstrations, repeat customer purchases, production activity and funded programs. These are better signals than a company's most ambitious timeline. By the 2040s, several pivots are normal: mechanisms to space robotics, test to flight operations, thermal engineering to habitats, or navigation to ocean autonomy."], "sourceIds": ["op-ucsd-ae", "op-bls-ae"]}]};